WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.760
- Good morning, and thank
you so much for coming.

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:16.030
I would also like to say good morning

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:17.990
and I wish you were here

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:21.400
to all of those who are
here with us in spirit

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:25.130
but just physically couldn't
make it to New Haven.

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:27.690
I want you to know if this is being filmed

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:28.910
and you're listening

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:31.190
that we're remembering you fondly.

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:33.420
We hold you tightly in our hearts,

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.990
and we wish you were here.

00:00:35.990 --> 00:00:37.393
You're really missed.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:41.780
Yesterday, through yesterday,

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:45.330
our panels dealt with the
decision to go coeducational

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:48.190
and its historical context.

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:51.660
Through Julia Pimsleur's wonderful movie

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.010
and the results of the survey,

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:54.940
we got a glimpse

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:59.420
of what it was like to be here in 1969.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:02.810
Today we're going to
plunge more fully into that

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:06.810
with a panel of six of us,

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:09.450
two from each class,

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:13.350
and our moderator is Anne Perkins.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:15.180
Anne was a trailblazer herself.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:19.160
She entered Yale in 1977,

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:20.690
a momentous year it turned out

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:23.703
for coeducation across the pond.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.480
She received her BA

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:29.570
and was a trailblazer

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:32.800
in that she was the first
woman editor-in-chief

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:34.629
of the Yale Daily News.

00:01:34.629 --> 00:01:35.507
- [Woman] Woo.

00:01:35.507 --> 00:01:36.829
(audience applauding)

00:01:36.829 --> 00:01:38.912
- [Woman] That's amazing.

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:46.540
- After Yale, Anne won

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:51.540
the prestigious Rhodes
Scholarship to Oxford.

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:56.620
In 1977, it took an Act of Parliament

00:01:56.810 --> 00:02:01.193
to change the Yale Trust
to allow women to attend

00:02:01.193 --> 00:02:03.713
as Rhodes Scholars.

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:08.920
Anne read Modern History
at Balliol College,

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:13.920
and Balliol College, talk
about male tradition.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:19.100
Balliol College had been single
sex from 1282 until 1977,

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:25.470
but back in the 1200s,

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:28.991
it was a woman who originally
funded Balliol College.

00:02:28.991 --> 00:02:30.100
(audience laughing)

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:31.740
- Amazing,

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:32.597
amazing.

00:02:32.597 --> 00:02:37.597
- After reading Modern History at Balliol,

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:41.320
Anne returned and got a master's

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:45.520
in Public Administration
from Harvard University.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:49.770
She then taught and worked
in educational policy

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:51.730
until at the age of 52,

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:53.520
she had what I like to think of

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:57.987
as a mid-life epiphany
and decided to get a PhD.

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:04.860
She juggled working while
pursuing a PhD in higher education

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:08.473
at the University of Massachusetts.

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:13.930
A paper which she had to
write for a retired course

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:17.317
eventually led to this wonderful book,

00:03:17.317 --> 00:03:19.840
"Yale Needs Women,"

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:21.770
and I love the title.

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:24.483
It's just fantastic.

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:29.930
Anne interviewed many, many of us,

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:32.300
and I spoke to many of them,

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:34.370
many of us who were interviewed by her.

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:36.380
Everyone was delighted with the process

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:39.070
and very pleased with the results.

00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:42.909
It's with great pleasure that
I introduce Anne Perkins.

00:03:42.909 --> 00:03:46.076
(audience applauding)

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:54.970
- Pam is very kind in calling

00:03:54.970 --> 00:03:57.930
my late life PhD an epiphany.

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:01.013
Really it was one of those
better late than never moments,

00:04:01.013 --> 00:04:02.100
(audience laughing)

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:04.040
and I did get my PhD,

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:05.560
and it's very odd for people

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:07.220
to call me Dr. Perkins,

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:10.070
so it is such an honor

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:13.330
to be here this weekend with all of you,

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:17.020
the first women of Yale College.

00:04:17.020 --> 00:04:20.330
The New York Times called
you the female versions

00:04:20.330 --> 00:04:22.067
of Nietzsche's ubermensch,

00:04:22.067 --> 00:04:23.820
(audience laughing)

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:26.223
whatever that means, superwomen,

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:31.180
but what was it like really
to be a woman student at Yale

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:35.080
during those tumultuous
early years of coeducation?

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:37.780
And the goal we're trying
to accomplish here today

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:41.130
is perhaps to give you a
chance to listen and learn

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:43.350
from other women as you
might not have been able

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:45.373
to do when you were a student here.

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:50.900
This is a different type of
session from the ones before

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:54.140
because in addition to
the experts up here,

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:56.360
every single woman sitting
in the chairs out there

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:59.190
who started at Yale is
an expert on this topic,

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:02.040
and so we're gonna organize
this a little differently,

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:04.160
but before I get to that game plan,

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:05.010
I just want to begin

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:06.993
by introducing our panelists.

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:13.150
As Pam said, you've heard
this weekend on topics

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:16.510
from before you got
here to, sorry, Billie,

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:18.650
to well after you arrived,

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:22.100
but our goal here today is
to go narrower and deeper,

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:26.740
four years only, 1969 to 1973,

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:29.410
and so the brief intro
bios that the panelists

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:33.370
and I put together emphasize
that part of their life.

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:35.940
You can think of it as a two
or three sentence glimpse

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:38.067
at who they were when they came to Yale

00:05:38.067 --> 00:05:40.740
and one or two of the
things that they did.

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:44.040
So I invite you to listen
to these bios for ways

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:46.760
that they intersect with your own,

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:49.840
ways that they offer perspectives
that you did not have

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:51.280
when you were at Yale,

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:54.130
and also for perspectives and experiences

00:05:54.130 --> 00:05:55.910
that are not up here on this panel

00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:58.913
and for which you might be able
to provide us some insights.

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:01.843
I'll start with the Class of '71,

00:06:03.650 --> 00:06:07.590
so Billie Tsien attended a
public high school in New Jersey

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:10.740
and spent her first two
years of college at Pembroke,

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:13.750
the former and now
vanished women's college

00:06:13.750 --> 00:06:15.123
at Brown University.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:17.627
Billie was in Pierson.

00:06:17.627 --> 00:06:19.563
(mumbling) is here to cheer for that,

00:06:20.950 --> 00:06:23.040
and majored in Fine Art.

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:25.160
One of her unforgettable Yale memories

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:28.670
was the December 1969 draft lottery

00:06:28.670 --> 00:06:30.770
and the sound of the lottery numbers

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:34.470
echoing through the Pierson
courtyard over speakers

00:06:34.470 --> 00:06:37.380
that students had set up for the purpose.

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:39.313
Some of you may remember that as well.

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:43.410
Kathy Murphy, also Class
of '71, came to Yale

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:45.700
as a junior after spending
her first two years

00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:47.950
of college at Mount Holyoke.

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:52.950
She grew up in Fremont,
Nebraska, population 18,000.

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:55.550
Is there really someone
else from Fremont here?

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:57.833
- [Woman] Nebraska.

00:06:57.833 --> 00:06:58.666
(audience laughing)

00:06:58.666 --> 00:07:01.310
- She was the first in her
family to go to college.

00:07:01.310 --> 00:07:04.370
At Yale, Kathy was in
Calhoun, Grace Hopper,

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:06.560
where she worked as a master's aide.

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:09.470
She majored in Study of the
City and met her husband,

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:12.863
a fellow Hounie, working the
Calhoun hotline over Mayday.

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:14.863
Class of '72,

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:19.940
Jerylle Kemp arrived
at Yale as a sophomore,

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:22.930
after starting college at
the University of Rochester,

00:07:22.930 --> 00:07:25.160
which I think I'm right
in saying you despised.

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:26.185
Is that correct?

00:07:26.185 --> 00:07:27.108
(laughing)

00:07:27.108 --> 00:07:28.420
(mumbling)

00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:30.360
For her first three years of high school,

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:33.400
Jerylle attended Booker
T. Washington in Atlanta,

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:35.910
Georgia's first public high school

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:38.610
open to African Americans.

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:39.900
For her last two years,

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:43.460
she attended Scarsdale
High School in New York.

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:45.940
Jerylle was active in the BSAY

00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:48.150
and was a member of the Yale Glee Club.

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:51.580
She majored in Afro-American
Studies with a concentration

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:53.073
in Political Science.

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:57.860
Kay Hill, Kay came to Yale
from a large public high school

00:07:57.860 --> 00:07:59.440
in West Hartford.

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:01.340
She spent her freshman year at Wellesley

00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:03.850
and arrived at Yale as a sophomore.

00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:08.400
At Yale, Kay was in JE,
majored in Psychology,

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:11.540
worked at Dwight Hall, and
taught ESL as a volunteer

00:08:11.540 --> 00:08:13.790
in the New Haven Public Schools.

00:08:13.790 --> 00:08:16.210
She lived off campus her
junior and senior years,

00:08:16.210 --> 00:08:17.250
including in a house

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:19.280
that she helped renovate in collaboration

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:22.083
with the Yale Student
Community Housing Corporation,

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:25.730
and our Class of '73 members.

00:08:25.730 --> 00:08:28.450
Joan Abrahamson attended
a large public high school

00:08:28.450 --> 00:08:31.400
in San Francisco and had
already been accepted

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:34.800
at Stanford when she
heard Yale was going coed.

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:38.622
She sent in her application
without telling her parents.

00:08:38.622 --> 00:08:40.560
(audience laughing)

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:42.640
At Yale, Joan was in Davenport College

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:45.480
and majored in Philosophy and Psychology.

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:49.410
She worked as a TA for Yale
Law Professor Charlie Reich,

00:08:49.410 --> 00:08:52.127
author of the best selling,
"The Greening of America,"

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:57.110
and last but definitely not
least, Cai Emmons, Class of '73,

00:08:57.110 --> 00:09:00.780
Cai graduated from a public
high school in suburban Boston.

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:04.440
She was in JE and majored
in Psychology and Drama.

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:07.490
She devoted much of her
time at Yale to theater,

00:09:07.490 --> 00:09:09.520
first acting, then writing plays,

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:10.770
one of which, Mergatroid,

00:09:10.770 --> 00:09:13.040
was staged in New York City.

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:14.960
During the strike her freshman year,

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:17.410
Cai became a more ardent feminist

00:09:17.410 --> 00:09:18.890
and as a result joined a group

00:09:18.890 --> 00:09:21.820
that helped New Haven women
obtain abortions in New York

00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:25.330
because they were still
illegal here in Connecticut,

00:09:25.330 --> 00:09:26.950
so that's our panel,

00:09:26.950 --> 00:09:28.540
and here's our game plan.

00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:31.140
We all know the drill of a typical panel.

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:32.540
The experts speak.

00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:34.300
The audience sits and listens,

00:09:34.300 --> 00:09:35.270
and then at the very end,

00:09:35.270 --> 00:09:37.160
the audience can ask questions

00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:39.840
and woe betide she who stands up and tries

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:42.000
to make a statement instead of a question

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:45.180
because you'll get the big
eye roll from the moderator.

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:48.230
We're gonna change
those rules in two ways.

00:09:48.230 --> 00:09:50.490
One is for each question,

00:09:50.490 --> 00:09:51.323
I'm gonna start

00:09:51.323 --> 00:09:54.230
with one or two or maybe
even three of the panelists,

00:09:54.230 --> 00:09:56.470
but then I'm gonna come
out to you as the audience,

00:09:56.470 --> 00:09:58.510
so you have a chance
to speak to that topic

00:09:58.510 --> 00:10:01.220
before we move on to the next,

00:10:01.220 --> 00:10:03.710
and then while you're more than welcome

00:10:03.710 --> 00:10:05.350
to ask questions,

00:10:05.350 --> 00:10:07.180
you are also welcome if you want

00:10:07.180 --> 00:10:09.550
to share your own
experience on the question,

00:10:09.550 --> 00:10:13.950
to answer it directly and
speak to that experience,

00:10:13.950 --> 00:10:15.730
so that's the good part.

00:10:15.730 --> 00:10:18.220
Here's the frustrating part.

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:22.690
There are 300 stories in
this room of what it was like

00:10:22.690 --> 00:10:24.390
to be a first woman at Yale,

00:10:24.390 --> 00:10:27.530
and every single one of
those stories deserves

00:10:27.530 --> 00:10:30.850
to be heard and honored in its entirety,

00:10:30.850 --> 00:10:34.626
and we have at this point
75 minutes to do so.

00:10:34.626 --> 00:10:35.700
(laughing)

00:10:35.700 --> 00:10:38.323
Mission kind of impossible,

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:40.040
but there are two things

00:10:40.040 --> 00:10:42.460
that we can do together this morning.

00:10:42.460 --> 00:10:45.620
One is for every woman
that chooses to speak,

00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:48.767
we can give her the gift of being heard

00:10:48.767 --> 00:10:50.293
and of being seen,

00:10:51.210 --> 00:10:52.700
and the other is we can think of this

00:10:52.700 --> 00:10:54.640
as the start of a conversation,

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:56.560
a conversation that I hope that all of you

00:10:56.560 --> 00:10:58.770
will continue not just today,

00:10:58.770 --> 00:11:00.560
but onward after this weekend

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:03.480
because it's a really
important conversation to hold

00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:05.960
and the experts in the entire world

00:11:05.960 --> 00:11:08.373
on this conversation are you.

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:14.890
We're gonna try to.

00:11:14.890 --> 00:11:16.070
Back to mission impossible,

00:11:16.070 --> 00:11:18.650
we're gonna try to include
as many voices as possible

00:11:18.650 --> 00:11:20.700
and cover a wide range of topics.

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:23.050
We came up with 12 questions.

00:11:23.050 --> 00:11:25.350
I cut them to nine last night.

00:11:25.350 --> 00:11:27.080
Don't even know if we're
gonna get to all nine.

00:11:27.080 --> 00:11:29.970
I'm gonna try to spend about
10 minutes per question,

00:11:29.970 --> 00:11:32.160
and so we'll see how that goes,

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:33.103
so let's begin.

00:11:34.580 --> 00:11:37.050
The first topic is identity.

00:11:37.050 --> 00:11:41.690
Students arrived at Yale in
1969 with many identities.

00:11:41.690 --> 00:11:44.610
What was your hometown,
your religion, your gender,

00:11:44.610 --> 00:11:47.670
your race and ethnicity, your sexuality?

00:11:47.670 --> 00:11:51.350
Were you the smart girl
in class, the athlete?

00:11:51.350 --> 00:11:53.280
And Billie, I'm gonna start with you.

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:55.670
What identity was most salient to you

00:11:55.670 --> 00:11:58.183
when you came to Yale and why?

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:04.260
- I think when I came to Yale,

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:07.350
I had no particular identity

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:10.180
except that I knew that
I wanted in some way

00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:11.820
or another to be different,

00:12:11.820 --> 00:12:14.830
and obviously I was different.

00:12:14.830 --> 00:12:18.260
However, I couldn't figure out how,

00:12:18.260 --> 00:12:23.020
and coming from a family
of engineers and scientists

00:12:23.020 --> 00:12:25.760
and the sort of whole
Chinese pressure of trying

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:26.660
to do the same thing,

00:12:26.660 --> 00:12:28.140
I decided that what I would do

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:31.015
would be to major in Art, but,

00:12:31.015 --> 00:12:32.010
(audience laughing)

00:12:32.010 --> 00:12:34.650
the interesting thing
was I came from Pembroke,

00:12:34.650 --> 00:12:36.570
and I just remember thinking,

00:12:36.570 --> 00:12:38.350
this is not the place for me,

00:12:38.350 --> 00:12:40.033
because every weekend,

00:12:41.350 --> 00:12:44.270
women would dress up in
dresses and wear corsages

00:12:44.270 --> 00:12:45.360
and go to football games,

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:47.930
and I thought, I can't be here.

00:12:47.930 --> 00:12:49.050
So when I came to Yale,

00:12:49.050 --> 00:12:51.020
the thing that was amazing for me

00:12:51.020 --> 00:12:53.460
was that there were more different kinds

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:56.700
of people doing more odd things

00:12:56.700 --> 00:12:59.370
in their own little ways

00:12:59.370 --> 00:13:01.240
than I could have every perceived

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:03.170
when I was at Pembroke at that time,

00:13:03.170 --> 00:13:06.313
and that for me was incredibly freeing.

00:13:07.650 --> 00:13:12.650
And I think what that taught me was,

00:13:13.550 --> 00:13:15.660
I didn't know how to get there,

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:17.300
and I didn't really know how

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:21.130
I was going to become different
in the way that I wanted to,

00:13:21.130 --> 00:13:23.370
but people were starting
to figure that out

00:13:23.370 --> 00:13:24.500
while they were here,

00:13:24.500 --> 00:13:27.770
and that's, I think, how I felt

00:13:29.210 --> 00:13:31.030
that I learned something
about my identity,

00:13:31.030 --> 00:13:33.980
although I didn't know the
answer when I was here.

00:13:33.980 --> 00:13:34.890
- Thank you,

00:13:34.890 --> 00:13:37.690
and I should note that
Billie avoided one thing,

00:13:37.690 --> 00:13:39.860
which is because I'm trying
to keep track of time,

00:13:39.860 --> 00:13:41.740
I have a lovely, I'm sorry.

00:13:41.740 --> 00:13:42.800
That was so sexist.

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:45.700
I have a young timekeeper in the back row

00:13:45.700 --> 00:13:48.683
who's gonna do this for me
if anyone hits two minutes,

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:51.150
and so just to let you know,

00:13:51.150 --> 00:13:52.913
but Billie avoided the yellow card.

00:13:54.350 --> 00:13:57.860
Joan, you want to jump in
and give us the perspective

00:13:57.860 --> 00:13:58.830
as a freshman woman,

00:13:58.830 --> 00:14:00.810
which is almost as obnoxious as asking you

00:14:00.810 --> 00:14:02.647
the opinion of a woman, but--

00:14:04.086 --> 00:14:08.000
- Yeah, I'm a fifth
generation San Franciscan,

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:11.400
and for me that really was my identity,

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:15.910
and I had never thought about
living on the East Coast

00:14:15.910 --> 00:14:17.163
or anything like that.

00:14:18.059 --> 00:14:22.390
I just was very happy and
involved in California,

00:14:22.390 --> 00:14:27.300
both in nature, a lot of
hiking and loved the Sierras,

00:14:27.300 --> 00:14:29.319
and I wrote poetry,

00:14:29.319 --> 00:14:32.760
and I was in my own kind of world,

00:14:32.760 --> 00:14:37.010
but I was very social justice oriented,

00:14:37.010 --> 00:14:38.900
and I was the youth representative

00:14:38.900 --> 00:14:42.530
on the Human Rights Commission
when I was in high school,

00:14:42.530 --> 00:14:44.610
so in Grace Cathedral is where we met.

00:14:44.610 --> 00:14:46.060
It was pretty amazing,

00:14:46.060 --> 00:14:50.260
so anyway, my dad really
wanted me to go to Stanford

00:14:50.260 --> 00:14:51.740
'cause he had gone there.

00:14:51.740 --> 00:14:52.980
I was admitted to Stanford.

00:14:52.980 --> 00:14:54.600
Everyone was happy.

00:14:54.600 --> 00:14:56.030
I didn't want to go.

00:14:56.030 --> 00:15:00.010
I was like, I want to go
somewhere no one knows me.

00:15:00.010 --> 00:15:03.680
I want to go somewhere where
I can find out who I am

00:15:03.680 --> 00:15:07.760
without everybody
knowing already who I am,

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:12.760
and when I told my parents at dinner

00:15:14.150 --> 00:15:15.580
that I was going to Yale,

00:15:15.580 --> 00:15:16.620
they were furious.

00:15:16.620 --> 00:15:19.720
My dad, he wouldn't talk to me for a day.

00:15:19.720 --> 00:15:21.380
He was really upset,

00:15:21.380 --> 00:15:23.400
and any rate, he got used to it,

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:24.777
but he would do things like,

00:15:24.777 --> 00:15:26.367
"Do you know what their song is?

00:15:26.367 --> 00:15:27.816
"Boolah, boolah,"

00:15:27.816 --> 00:15:29.900
(audience laughing)

00:15:29.900 --> 00:15:32.447
and I said, that's not true (mumbling),

00:15:32.447 --> 00:15:35.447
(audience laughing)

00:15:36.730 --> 00:15:39.210
so they dropped me off at
the San Francisco airport.

00:15:39.210 --> 00:15:43.810
I'm with my green footlocker
I had had at camp every year,

00:15:43.810 --> 00:15:45.930
and I get on the plane.

00:15:45.930 --> 00:15:48.900
I get off at JFK in the TWA Terminal,

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:50.530
and I look at the Saarinen building,

00:15:50.530 --> 00:15:52.890
and I am like blown away, you know?

00:15:52.890 --> 00:15:54.960
I'd never seen anything like that,

00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:59.130
so then I flag down the
Connecticut limousine,

00:15:59.130 --> 00:16:02.650
and they put my footlocker
on the top of the van

00:16:02.650 --> 00:16:05.010
and drive me here and drop me

00:16:05.010 --> 00:16:06.830
right outside the old campus,

00:16:06.830 --> 00:16:08.003
right across from the Green

00:16:08.003 --> 00:16:10.350
at the gate there,

00:16:10.350 --> 00:16:11.670
and I'm there with my footlocker,

00:16:11.670 --> 00:16:15.730
thinking, how the hell am I
gonna get this thing anywhere?

00:16:15.730 --> 00:16:16.977
So I leave it there,

00:16:16.977 --> 00:16:18.820
and I walk into the little campus

00:16:18.820 --> 00:16:20.810
and flag down a couple of guys who,

00:16:20.810 --> 00:16:25.130
they carried it up to Entryway Six,

00:16:25.130 --> 00:16:27.591
of course, the top floor or something,

00:16:27.591 --> 00:16:29.900
and I meet my roommates,

00:16:29.900 --> 00:16:33.330
and we walk over to Davenport
for the welcome speech,

00:16:33.330 --> 00:16:34.660
and of course the welcome speech--

00:16:34.660 --> 00:16:36.017
- [Anne] Joan, I'm just gonna warn you,

00:16:36.017 --> 00:16:37.930
you got the yellow card so if you could--

00:16:37.930 --> 00:16:38.763
- Oh my god--

00:16:38.763 --> 00:16:39.596
- [Anne] Wrap it up just
so we could hear one more--

00:16:39.596 --> 00:16:40.429
- Okay, well, then I'm not gonna tell you

00:16:40.429 --> 00:16:42.330
that story because--

00:16:42.330 --> 00:16:43.627
- [Anne] Oh, you got to
make sure to ask her after--

00:16:43.627 --> 00:16:46.613
- Not as important as this
because this is important.

00:16:47.500 --> 00:16:50.790
My only mentor and advisor
that I was assigned

00:16:50.790 --> 00:16:52.940
was Rulon Wells, who was in Davenport.

00:16:52.940 --> 00:16:54.350
I don't know if anyone's ever met him.

00:16:54.350 --> 00:16:56.700
He was a classicist,

00:16:56.700 --> 00:16:59.560
and I don't think he'd
ever seen a woman student

00:16:59.560 --> 00:17:02.360
before ever in his life,

00:17:02.360 --> 00:17:04.747
and so he walks in, and he says,

00:17:04.747 --> 00:17:07.210
"Now what would you like to major in?"

00:17:07.210 --> 00:17:09.384
And I said, forestry,

00:17:09.384 --> 00:17:11.790
(laughing)

00:17:11.790 --> 00:17:13.717
and Rulon Wells looks at me and says,

00:17:13.717 --> 00:17:15.626
"You can't major in Forestry.

00:17:15.626 --> 00:17:18.160
"There is no forestry classes."

00:17:18.160 --> 00:17:21.240
I said, well, Sage Hall
is famous for forestry.

00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:22.950
I wanted to be a forest ranger

00:17:22.950 --> 00:17:25.680
in one of those little ranger houses

00:17:25.680 --> 00:17:27.130
where you could write poetry,

00:17:28.911 --> 00:17:31.460
and he looked at me like I was an alien,

00:17:31.460 --> 00:17:34.700
and he was just like flabbergasted,

00:17:34.700 --> 00:17:35.533
and when I left,

00:17:35.533 --> 00:17:38.403
he was so happy that he
never had to see me again.

00:17:39.990 --> 00:17:41.440
And then I wanted to say

00:17:41.440 --> 00:17:42.920
that I was asked so often,

00:17:42.920 --> 00:17:44.130
I know, but it'll be fast.

00:17:44.130 --> 00:17:46.200
For the woman's point of view

00:17:46.200 --> 00:17:49.980
that in my Classics class as a freshman,

00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:52.730
I finally started enjoying it,

00:17:52.730 --> 00:17:54.350
so I would say, oh, well,

00:17:54.350 --> 00:17:55.930
the woman's point of view of Odysseus

00:17:55.930 --> 00:17:58.610
is he was tied to the mast,

00:17:58.610 --> 00:18:00.850
and they said the sirens
were luring him to his death.

00:18:00.850 --> 00:18:04.280
No, I said, the sirens
were simply singing.

00:18:04.280 --> 00:18:05.340
They were in the water.

00:18:05.340 --> 00:18:06.173
They were friends.

00:18:06.173 --> 00:18:07.006
They were singing.

00:18:07.006 --> 00:18:07.947
They were happy.

00:18:07.947 --> 00:18:09.260
(audience laughing)

00:18:09.260 --> 00:18:13.300
I said, listen.

00:18:13.300 --> 00:18:16.251
I said, it's not our fault
he wanted to jump in.

00:18:16.251 --> 00:18:19.251
(audience laughing)

00:18:26.120 --> 00:18:27.560
- All right, anyone in the audience

00:18:27.560 --> 00:18:30.440
who wants to speak to this?

00:18:30.440 --> 00:18:31.750
Who can top that story?

00:18:31.750 --> 00:18:33.190
'Cause I bet you can't.

00:18:33.190 --> 00:18:35.570
Who wants to speak to
this question of identity

00:18:35.570 --> 00:18:39.400
and when I'm thinking
about it, I'm curious.

00:18:39.400 --> 00:18:41.070
Were there aspects of your identity

00:18:41.070 --> 00:18:42.550
that were in conflict,

00:18:42.550 --> 00:18:44.590
that you couldn't be
both at the same time?

00:18:44.590 --> 00:18:46.360
Or where the aspects that changed

00:18:46.360 --> 00:18:47.793
while you were here at Yale?

00:18:50.270 --> 00:18:51.103
Yeah.

00:18:52.840 --> 00:18:53.673
Carol.

00:18:54.560 --> 00:18:57.710
Oh, and if you could
start by saying your name

00:18:57.710 --> 00:18:59.730
and your class just so
everyone can know you

00:18:59.730 --> 00:19:00.960
if they don't already.

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:04.490
- Yeah, hi, I'm Carol Story
Johnson, Class of 1973,

00:19:05.840 --> 00:19:07.830
and I'm gonna put a plug in.

00:19:07.830 --> 00:19:10.560
If you haven't read the
book, "Yale Needs Women,"

00:19:10.560 --> 00:19:12.253
you need to read it.

00:19:12.253 --> 00:19:13.426
It's really terrific, terrific.

00:19:13.426 --> 00:19:14.468
(audience applauding)

00:19:14.468 --> 00:19:16.080
- [Anne] Thanks, Carol.

00:19:16.080 --> 00:19:19.230
- So real quickly, so here I
am, an African American woman,

00:19:19.230 --> 00:19:22.290
Class of 1973, and I come,

00:19:22.290 --> 00:19:25.970
and I have a lot of interesting thoughts

00:19:25.970 --> 00:19:28.790
about whether or not I'm
identifying with women

00:19:28.790 --> 00:19:31.730
or with the African American community,

00:19:31.730 --> 00:19:33.350
and actually early on,

00:19:33.350 --> 00:19:35.570
and I'm sure Jerylle's
gonna speak to some of this,

00:19:35.570 --> 00:19:39.740
so, Jerylle, certainly
I'm gonna corroborate

00:19:39.740 --> 00:19:41.430
some of our mutual story,

00:19:41.430 --> 00:19:44.710
but connected with the Black
Student Alliance at Yale,

00:19:44.710 --> 00:19:47.503
and it was actually a kind of a haven

00:19:47.503 --> 00:19:51.920
for folks like me who were
looking for identity pieces,

00:19:51.920 --> 00:19:54.180
and it was enormously important,

00:19:54.180 --> 00:19:57.323
and I think it actually kind
of protected me in certain ways

00:19:57.323 --> 00:20:01.330
from some of the other things
that many of the other women

00:20:01.330 --> 00:20:03.390
on campus were going through.

00:20:03.390 --> 00:20:05.000
Anne talks a lot in her book

00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:09.120
about the sexual harassment pieces

00:20:09.120 --> 00:20:10.620
that went on here at Yale,

00:20:10.620 --> 00:20:12.950
and I was actually curious from the panel

00:20:12.950 --> 00:20:15.100
whether or not folks
on the panel were aware

00:20:15.100 --> 00:20:16.840
of all of that that was going on,

00:20:16.840 --> 00:20:17.730
so that would be my question--

00:20:17.730 --> 00:20:19.672
- That's question number seven, yeah.

00:20:19.672 --> 00:20:21.170
(laughing)

00:20:21.170 --> 00:20:22.470
Thanks so much, Carol.

00:20:22.470 --> 00:20:25.210
Anyone else who wants to
speak to the identity question

00:20:25.210 --> 00:20:26.303
before we move on?

00:20:27.950 --> 00:20:29.033
See right there?

00:20:35.740 --> 00:20:37.463
- Meredith Wright, '71,

00:20:39.050 --> 00:20:43.180
my high school sort of
social life revolved around

00:20:43.180 --> 00:20:45.070
something particularly odd,

00:20:45.070 --> 00:20:50.070
which was Balkan or
international folk dancing, and--

00:20:50.900 --> 00:20:52.778
- [Anne] Are you gonna give
us a performance please--

00:20:52.778 --> 00:20:57.778
- No, but within a month of
arriving as a transfer student,

00:20:59.900 --> 00:21:04.900
there was a poster up
advertising for auditions

00:21:05.150 --> 00:21:07.260
for the Yale Slavic Chorus,

00:21:07.260 --> 00:21:09.289
and I knew I had found my home.

00:21:09.289 --> 00:21:11.100
(laughing)

00:21:11.100 --> 00:21:11.933
- That's great.

00:21:11.933 --> 00:21:12.992
Thanks so much.

00:21:12.992 --> 00:21:14.700
(audience applauding)

00:21:14.700 --> 00:21:16.880
Okay, we're gonna move
on to the next questions,

00:21:16.880 --> 00:21:19.220
and I'm sorry if that's
one you wanted to talk to.

00:21:19.220 --> 00:21:22.360
Please continue to speak
to one another about it.

00:21:22.360 --> 00:21:23.580
This one's on engagement.

00:21:23.580 --> 00:21:25.210
We're gonna go a little broader,

00:21:25.210 --> 00:21:27.363
and, Kay, I'm gonna start with you.

00:21:28.865 --> 00:21:30.330
This is an impossible question,

00:21:30.330 --> 00:21:32.300
but I don't have to answer it.

00:21:32.300 --> 00:21:35.910
What was your greatest
passion when you were at Yale?

00:21:35.910 --> 00:21:38.810
What mattered most to you and why?

00:21:38.810 --> 00:21:42.180
- Right, I would say what mattered to me

00:21:42.180 --> 00:21:45.890
was really being involved in the community

00:21:45.890 --> 00:21:48.183
and engagement and service.

00:21:49.120 --> 00:21:51.890
Along with a handful of other women,

00:21:51.890 --> 00:21:56.200
before Yale officially
started in the fall,

00:21:56.200 --> 00:22:00.190
we spent the summer
working for Dwight Hall,

00:22:00.190 --> 00:22:05.190
and I taught Art and ESL with
the children on Grand Avenue

00:22:06.380 --> 00:22:08.620
who were mostly Puerto Rican.

00:22:08.620 --> 00:22:10.290
I loved using my Spanish.

00:22:10.290 --> 00:22:12.040
I loved the children,

00:22:12.040 --> 00:22:15.320
and actually it was pivotal in my career.

00:22:15.320 --> 00:22:18.890
My very first job after
graduating from Yale

00:22:18.890 --> 00:22:22.190
was opening the first
bilingual daycare center,

00:22:22.190 --> 00:22:24.560
the (mumbling) Center in New Haven,

00:22:24.560 --> 00:22:25.930
and I was hired by Junta,

00:22:25.930 --> 00:22:29.300
for whom I had worked
before I ever started here.

00:22:29.300 --> 00:22:32.880
I look back with amazement that at 21

00:22:32.880 --> 00:22:36.300
I thought I could get
funding for a daycare center,

00:22:36.300 --> 00:22:41.300
hire the bilingual local
teachers, put in the tiny toilets,

00:22:41.560 --> 00:22:45.130
and run the connection
to the main water line

00:22:45.130 --> 00:22:47.160
where it turned out we were going

00:22:47.160 --> 00:22:51.070
through some of the tombs of the founders

00:22:51.070 --> 00:22:53.410
of the church where we were,

00:22:53.410 --> 00:22:55.023
but we diverted.

00:22:56.360 --> 00:22:58.660
I also, it was alluded to.

00:22:58.660 --> 00:23:00.290
Along with a group of six others,

00:23:00.290 --> 00:23:01.550
we renovated a house

00:23:01.550 --> 00:23:05.370
where a child had acquired
lead paint poisoning

00:23:05.370 --> 00:23:07.970
and stripped the lead paint.

00:23:07.970 --> 00:23:10.370
Then we lived in the house ourselves.

00:23:10.370 --> 00:23:12.800
When it wasn't ready on time,

00:23:12.800 --> 00:23:17.350
Yale let us live in an office
building on Ashman Street.

00:23:17.350 --> 00:23:19.100
Judy (mumbling) will remember that.

00:23:19.100 --> 00:23:21.480
She was part of that group of seven,

00:23:21.480 --> 00:23:24.080
and then in my senior year, I taught.

00:23:24.080 --> 00:23:26.680
I arranged all my classes and taught ESL

00:23:26.680 --> 00:23:31.680
as a volunteer in the
New Haven public schools,

00:23:32.040 --> 00:23:36.630
and then I lived off campus
junior year and senior year,

00:23:36.630 --> 00:23:39.670
and I was hired then to be a teacher

00:23:39.670 --> 00:23:41.930
and had a 35 year career,

00:23:41.930 --> 00:23:44.730
ending it as a central
office administrator

00:23:44.730 --> 00:23:47.210
in charge of a number of programs,

00:23:47.210 --> 00:23:49.760
and then I retired several years ago.

00:23:49.760 --> 00:23:52.530
Looking back, I think all of this passion

00:23:52.530 --> 00:23:55.600
for working in the community is admirable,

00:23:55.600 --> 00:23:57.220
and it's something we would like

00:23:57.220 --> 00:24:00.030
to see perhaps more of today,

00:24:00.030 --> 00:24:03.120
but I also think in reflecting about it

00:24:03.120 --> 00:24:06.330
that it's interesting
that I was so unaware

00:24:06.330 --> 00:24:10.580
that I was in a place of special
privilege and opportunity.

00:24:10.580 --> 00:24:12.530
To come to Yale and not be able

00:24:12.530 --> 00:24:16.820
to wait to get away is just odd,

00:24:16.820 --> 00:24:21.760
and I think the chance to savor all

00:24:21.760 --> 00:24:23.960
the academics that were being offered,

00:24:23.960 --> 00:24:26.490
all the enrichment, all the culture,

00:24:26.490 --> 00:24:29.010
and having your meals prepared for you

00:24:30.250 --> 00:24:31.990
in a beautiful setting,

00:24:31.990 --> 00:24:36.430
I just find it kind of
amusing and interesting,

00:24:36.430 --> 00:24:38.860
but it's probably also a sign of the times

00:24:38.860 --> 00:24:43.793
that we were so passionate
about helping other people.

00:24:45.040 --> 00:24:46.070
- Thanks, Kay.

00:24:46.070 --> 00:24:47.630
Cai, you want to speak to that one too?

00:24:47.630 --> 00:24:49.510
- About engagement, okay,

00:24:49.510 --> 00:24:52.410
my time at Yale was sort of bifurcated

00:24:52.410 --> 00:24:57.410
by a semester off in the second
part of my sophomore year.

00:24:57.830 --> 00:25:01.080
I came to Yale very
passionate about theater.

00:25:01.080 --> 00:25:02.370
I'd been acting,

00:25:02.370 --> 00:25:05.303
and eventually I would
begin writing plays,

00:25:06.240 --> 00:25:09.600
but my interest in that kind of waned

00:25:09.600 --> 00:25:12.450
about halfway through my time here.

00:25:12.450 --> 00:25:14.550
I applied for the five year BA.

00:25:14.550 --> 00:25:17.563
I don't know if anybody here
ended up going on that program,

00:25:18.731 --> 00:25:22.120
oh, and got accepted to teach English

00:25:22.120 --> 00:25:24.020
in the Marshall Islands

00:25:24.020 --> 00:25:28.150
where I was going to have
to learn Marshallese,

00:25:28.150 --> 00:25:29.920
and they asked me whether I wanted to be

00:25:29.920 --> 00:25:31.570
on one of the big islands

00:25:31.570 --> 00:25:33.783
or one of the outer atolls,

00:25:34.900 --> 00:25:36.390
so this happened,

00:25:36.390 --> 00:25:40.200
and, oh, by the way, the
interview for that was,

00:25:40.200 --> 00:25:43.810
as I recall, there was a
long table with 12 people.

00:25:43.810 --> 00:25:46.570
And you had to find your own job,

00:25:46.570 --> 00:25:49.550
and one of the psychiatrists

00:25:49.550 --> 00:25:51.817
on the interviewing committee said,

00:25:51.817 --> 00:25:53.617
"How would you deal with the situation

00:25:53.617 --> 00:25:54.677
"if you were on the beach

00:25:54.677 --> 00:25:55.937
"in the Marshall Islands,

00:25:55.937 --> 00:26:00.017
"and you had taken off all your clothes,

00:26:00.017 --> 00:26:04.097
"and then you went swimming,

00:26:04.097 --> 00:26:05.157
"and when you came out,

00:26:05.157 --> 00:26:07.225
"there were no clothes there for you?"

00:26:07.225 --> 00:26:08.210
(audience laughing)

00:26:08.210 --> 00:26:10.820
I just thought it was really, well,

00:26:10.820 --> 00:26:13.289
we all understand the context of this.

00:26:13.289 --> 00:26:15.910
(audience laughing)

00:26:15.910 --> 00:26:16.980
- [Anne] Wow.

00:26:16.980 --> 00:26:19.180
- And I can't recall
how I answered, but I--

00:26:20.870 --> 00:26:22.410
- You needed Joan to answer it for you.

00:26:22.410 --> 00:26:24.600
She would have had some sharp reply.

00:26:24.600 --> 00:26:25.750
- Yeah, right, exactly,

00:26:27.260 --> 00:26:29.550
so but at the same time,

00:26:29.550 --> 00:26:31.190
one of the people in the drama school

00:26:31.190 --> 00:26:33.850
that I'd been acting with,
one of the directors,

00:26:33.850 --> 00:26:38.040
had asked me if I wanted to
be in the spring semester

00:26:38.040 --> 00:26:41.660
of my sophomore year in a
movie on the Costa Brava,

00:26:41.660 --> 00:26:44.150
and he had written this part for me,

00:26:44.150 --> 00:26:47.090
and so if I went to the movie,

00:26:47.090 --> 00:26:49.430
I couldn't go to the five
year BA and vice versa.

00:26:49.430 --> 00:26:53.070
So it was really a very
difficult decision at the time,

00:26:53.070 --> 00:26:55.290
but I thought, well, going
to the Marshall Islands

00:26:55.290 --> 00:26:59.450
wasn't really within
something I had imagined,

00:26:59.450 --> 00:27:00.371
and it didn't seem like it was leading

00:27:00.371 --> 00:27:04.076
to a career path, and so--

00:27:04.076 --> 00:27:04.960
- [Woman] So you did it?

00:27:04.960 --> 00:27:07.430
- So I ended up deciding to go

00:27:07.430 --> 00:27:09.920
and be in the movie on the Costa Brava.

00:27:09.920 --> 00:27:12.693
However, he never got his funding,

00:27:13.640 --> 00:27:17.030
and so I ended up being out of school

00:27:17.030 --> 00:27:18.350
for the sophomore year,

00:27:19.880 --> 00:27:21.100
spring semester sophomore year,

00:27:21.100 --> 00:27:22.510
which was actually really good.

00:27:22.510 --> 00:27:23.730
It was very clarifying,

00:27:23.730 --> 00:27:25.700
and when I came back to Yale,

00:27:25.700 --> 00:27:27.450
this is all about engagement.

00:27:27.450 --> 00:27:29.920
For the latter two years,
I was very engaged--

00:27:29.920 --> 00:27:30.753
- [Anne] I am just gonna let you know

00:27:30.753 --> 00:27:31.710
you got the yellow card,

00:27:31.710 --> 00:27:33.840
so maybe give us one more
quick story so we can--

00:27:33.840 --> 00:27:36.340
- Yeah, I was very engaged in psychology

00:27:36.340 --> 00:27:38.690
and community psychology in particular,

00:27:38.690 --> 00:27:41.940
and so I sort of ended up graduating

00:27:41.940 --> 00:27:44.820
with both a drama
degree, a theater degree,

00:27:44.820 --> 00:27:46.350
and a psychology degree,

00:27:46.350 --> 00:27:48.130
and then that was huge choice,

00:27:48.130 --> 00:27:49.550
but we won't go there yet.

00:27:49.550 --> 00:27:51.190
- Thanks so much.

00:27:51.190 --> 00:27:54.360
Anyone in the audience, what
was your passion at Yale?

00:27:54.360 --> 00:27:56.780
What did you care most about?

00:27:56.780 --> 00:27:59.973
We've got the mic coming right to you.

00:28:03.590 --> 00:28:08.590
- Hi, I'm Dahlia Rudavsky,
Class of '73, Branford,

00:28:08.600 --> 00:28:11.360
and I came to Yale with the identity

00:28:11.360 --> 00:28:13.910
of being a student radical somehow.

00:28:13.910 --> 00:28:15.290
From my high school days,

00:28:15.290 --> 00:28:19.030
I had organized our anti-war club

00:28:19.030 --> 00:28:23.070
at my high school in
Newton, Massachusetts,

00:28:23.070 --> 00:28:25.603
and Yale did not disappoint.

00:28:26.800 --> 00:28:30.470
Within the first month, I think,
we were on a hunger strike.

00:28:30.470 --> 00:28:33.480
I don't know if you
remember the moratorium,

00:28:33.480 --> 00:28:38.280
and I remember sitting
in front of Vanderbilt,

00:28:38.280 --> 00:28:42.320
on the third or fourth day of
this hunger strike, very weak.

00:28:42.320 --> 00:28:45.280
We were on a juice fast,

00:28:45.280 --> 00:28:48.060
and that was one of my first memories,

00:28:48.060 --> 00:28:51.780
and then, soon thereafter, Mrs. Williams,

00:28:51.780 --> 00:28:54.203
who was a dining hall worker,

00:28:55.440 --> 00:29:00.440
was fired for throwing some
orange juice at a supervisor

00:29:00.900 --> 00:29:03.360
who had ordered her around,

00:29:03.360 --> 00:29:06.820
and there was SDS on campus

00:29:06.820 --> 00:29:11.290
organized a Reinstate
Mrs. Williams campaign,

00:29:11.290 --> 00:29:12.920
and then the Panthers came to town,

00:29:12.920 --> 00:29:16.840
and from there we never
had a spring semester.

00:29:16.840 --> 00:29:21.840
So at the same time, I had
some great literature courses,

00:29:23.090 --> 00:29:26.410
which were a total,
the other side of life,

00:29:26.410 --> 00:29:28.960
so Yale was wonderful in that way,

00:29:28.960 --> 00:29:32.440
and I also want to thank Anne
for putting it all together

00:29:32.440 --> 00:29:37.440
so we can see how our
individual experiences

00:29:37.650 --> 00:29:41.718
were reflected in the zeitgeist.

00:29:41.718 --> 00:29:43.355
- Thanks, Dahlia.

00:29:43.355 --> 00:29:45.603
Anyone else would like (mumbling).

00:29:48.360 --> 00:29:50.010
- Inject a little levity here.

00:29:50.010 --> 00:29:52.350
I can't really say it was a passion,

00:29:52.350 --> 00:29:55.031
but I really wanted to be on Jeopardy.

00:29:55.031 --> 00:29:57.580
(audience laughing)

00:29:57.580 --> 00:29:58.413
- [Anne] Can you repeat that?

00:29:58.413 --> 00:29:59.870
I'm not sure everyone heard.

00:29:59.870 --> 00:30:01.670
- I really wanted to be on Jeopardy,

00:30:02.614 --> 00:30:03.963
so I trained.

00:30:05.234 --> 00:30:06.067
I was in Branford.

00:30:06.067 --> 00:30:07.430
I'm Donna Weston by the way,

00:30:07.430 --> 00:30:09.320
and I had three guy friends,

00:30:09.320 --> 00:30:11.700
and I would play home
Jeopardy in their room

00:30:11.700 --> 00:30:13.080
many times a week,

00:30:13.080 --> 00:30:14.830
and I figured that I
had a pretty good chance

00:30:14.830 --> 00:30:16.560
of getting on 'cause I had this gimmick,

00:30:16.560 --> 00:30:19.120
which was I was in the first
class of women at Yale.

00:30:19.120 --> 00:30:20.810
So indeed, I went down to New York,

00:30:20.810 --> 00:30:22.070
and I tried out,

00:30:22.070 --> 00:30:23.250
and they called me back.

00:30:23.250 --> 00:30:24.490
I brought three outfits

00:30:24.490 --> 00:30:27.533
'cause they filmed them all seriatim.

00:30:28.500 --> 00:30:30.460
I won the first day.

00:30:30.460 --> 00:30:32.430
I did not bet enough money

00:30:32.430 --> 00:30:36.560
the second day on final
Jeopardy, so I lost,

00:30:36.560 --> 00:30:39.360
and I emerged with $1,820,

00:30:39.360 --> 00:30:42.403
the Encyclopedia
Americana, and a home game.

00:30:42.403 --> 00:30:46.653
(audience laughing and applauding)

00:30:52.380 --> 00:30:53.610
- I don't know if you all know this.

00:30:53.610 --> 00:30:55.780
Brenda Jubin, who was
on the panel yesterday,

00:30:55.780 --> 00:30:59.130
when she got appointed
as the first woman dean

00:30:59.130 --> 00:31:00.410
of a residential college,

00:31:00.410 --> 00:31:03.880
she got called up by the
head of What's My Line.

00:31:03.880 --> 00:31:05.730
I don't know if you remember that,

00:31:05.730 --> 00:31:07.370
and she went on as the first,

00:31:07.370 --> 00:31:11.363
and no one could guess what she was.

00:31:12.420 --> 00:31:13.470
Okay, one more,

00:31:13.470 --> 00:31:15.820
and then we'll, right
there with the red shirt.

00:31:19.100 --> 00:31:22.850
- Hi, Laura Handman, '73, JE.

00:31:22.850 --> 00:31:24.990
Following up on your story,

00:31:24.990 --> 00:31:28.120
I had gotten into the
University of Santa Cruz

00:31:28.120 --> 00:31:30.260
and Reed College in Oregon,

00:31:30.260 --> 00:31:31.810
and I wasn't gonna come to Yale,

00:31:31.810 --> 00:31:33.480
and my parents were furious

00:31:33.480 --> 00:31:34.910
and made me come to Yale,

00:31:34.910 --> 00:31:37.700
and I think about how different
my life would have been

00:31:37.700 --> 00:31:40.910
if I had gone to those two other schools,

00:31:40.910 --> 00:31:42.603
and I followed on.

00:31:44.030 --> 00:31:46.280
Apropos of the Black Panthers,

00:31:46.280 --> 00:31:50.595
I did get arrested three
weeks into our freshman year

00:31:50.595 --> 00:31:51.550
(audience laughing)

00:31:51.550 --> 00:31:55.110
for sitting in at a welfare center.

00:31:55.110 --> 00:31:57.830
They were cutting off the
benefits of welfare moms

00:31:57.830 --> 00:31:59.120
or reducing them,

00:31:59.120 --> 00:32:02.297
and there came a moment when they said,

00:32:02.297 --> 00:32:04.867
"Those of you who want
to get arrested, stay.

00:32:04.867 --> 00:32:06.070
"Those who don't, leave,"

00:32:06.070 --> 00:32:08.880
and all the Yalies but me and one other,

00:32:08.880 --> 00:32:12.250
Gwen, I don't know if
you're here, got arrested.

00:32:12.250 --> 00:32:13.960
And I was 17,

00:32:13.960 --> 00:32:17.790
so the dean of JE had to be my guardian--

00:32:17.790 --> 00:32:18.623
- [Woman] Oh my god--

00:32:18.623 --> 00:32:19.977
- And to say that they were wondering,

00:32:19.977 --> 00:32:22.200
"Why did we get these women at Yale,"

00:32:22.200 --> 00:32:23.930
(audience laughing)

00:32:23.930 --> 00:32:26.320
I think went through his mind.

00:32:26.320 --> 00:32:27.947
- Thank you,

00:32:27.947 --> 00:32:31.114
(audience applauding)

00:32:33.970 --> 00:32:38.540
so when I interviewed many
of you for my research,

00:32:38.540 --> 00:32:40.270
a topic that came up again and again

00:32:40.270 --> 00:32:42.280
is this topic of friendship,

00:32:42.280 --> 00:32:43.283
so we're gonna switch to that,

00:32:43.283 --> 00:32:45.780
and, Jerylle, I want to start with you.

00:32:45.780 --> 00:32:48.410
How did you meet your
closest friends at Yale,

00:32:48.410 --> 00:32:51.090
and what did you have in common with them,

00:32:51.090 --> 00:32:52.730
and how were you different?

00:32:52.730 --> 00:32:56.200
And sort of a follow up just to give you

00:32:56.200 --> 00:32:57.820
way too many questions to answer at once,

00:32:57.820 --> 00:32:59.120
were you ever lonely here?

00:33:02.330 --> 00:33:03.163
- Good luck.

00:33:04.270 --> 00:33:06.470
- How did I meet my
closest friends at Yale?

00:33:07.620 --> 00:33:09.923
As a member of the BSAY,

00:33:11.380 --> 00:33:15.150
and as an African
American woman on campus,

00:33:15.150 --> 00:33:19.670
I had the opportunity to engage

00:33:19.670 --> 00:33:23.543
with the entire black
community that was on campus.

00:33:24.850 --> 00:33:26.860
It made a difference.

00:33:26.860 --> 00:33:29.630
I just, in the past few
days, have come to understand

00:33:29.630 --> 00:33:33.610
what a difference it made
because it's been made clear

00:33:33.610 --> 00:33:38.610
to me that many of us
didn't have an easy time

00:33:38.990 --> 00:33:42.053
establishing friendships on Yale's campus,

00:33:43.200 --> 00:33:45.573
and that made the experience much harder.

00:33:46.900 --> 00:33:50.740
Within the BSAY, we had the good fortune

00:33:50.740 --> 00:33:54.970
to have some wonderful
people who made participating

00:33:54.970 --> 00:33:57.250
in that organization a priority.

00:33:57.250 --> 00:34:00.750
They generously gave of their
time and their resources

00:34:00.750 --> 00:34:05.395
and their physical facilities.

00:34:05.395 --> 00:34:10.395
We made friends by
participating in dinners

00:34:11.950 --> 00:34:16.870
in our colleges or in Berkeley College

00:34:16.870 --> 00:34:21.870
because Room 597 was the
home of Stephanie Brown

00:34:22.160 --> 00:34:26.410
and then Sheila Jackson,

00:34:26.410 --> 00:34:30.510
a networking hub for the
black community on campus.

00:34:30.510 --> 00:34:32.550
We had Afro-America House,

00:34:32.550 --> 00:34:36.010
which gave us a place to locate.

00:34:36.010 --> 00:34:39.770
There were the protests, which
we all went through together,

00:34:39.770 --> 00:34:43.810
and had experiences that
we never anticipated

00:34:43.810 --> 00:34:46.320
as part of our undergraduate experience,

00:34:46.320 --> 00:34:49.180
but all of it gave us
opportunities to grow

00:34:49.180 --> 00:34:50.763
and to get to know each other.

00:34:51.680 --> 00:34:55.720
What did we share in addition
to our racial identity?

00:34:55.720 --> 00:34:58.370
I think it's fair to say we shared

00:34:59.800 --> 00:35:04.070
the sense of being part of
an experimental generation.

00:35:04.070 --> 00:35:08.480
Many of us became involved

00:35:09.880 --> 00:35:13.080
as the diversity element in institutions

00:35:13.080 --> 00:35:16.630
which had never had persons of color,

00:35:16.630 --> 00:35:20.423
students of color, present on campus.

00:35:25.040 --> 00:35:28.530
I guess that came through
most clearly at Yale

00:35:28.530 --> 00:35:30.600
during the Mayday proceedings,

00:35:30.600 --> 00:35:33.570
when everybody had to make a decision

00:35:33.570 --> 00:35:37.193
about whether we were
gonna go home or stay,

00:35:38.450 --> 00:35:42.000
and I remember very
clearly a big discussion,

00:35:42.000 --> 00:35:46.660
which was held on campus
at the Afro-America House,

00:35:46.660 --> 00:35:51.000
where it became clear to me
that many of the students

00:35:53.030 --> 00:35:56.903
made participating in the
politics of the day a priority.

00:35:57.850 --> 00:35:59.610
Many of the students felt

00:35:59.610 --> 00:36:01.850
that they were at Yale to get a degree

00:36:01.850 --> 00:36:03.450
and didn't have time

00:36:04.520 --> 00:36:05.890
and didn't want to run the risk

00:36:05.890 --> 00:36:07.823
of being on campus during Mayday,

00:36:08.700 --> 00:36:11.480
but it also occurred to
me that the black students

00:36:11.480 --> 00:36:16.313
were the nexus between the
community and the university.

00:36:17.590 --> 00:36:20.190
The community saw the black students

00:36:20.190 --> 00:36:21.833
as representatives of Yale.

00:36:23.020 --> 00:36:28.020
Yale saw the black students
as a conduit to the community.

00:36:29.090 --> 00:36:31.915
We thought we came here to get a degree.

00:36:31.915 --> 00:36:33.390
(audience laughing)

00:36:33.390 --> 00:36:34.760
- [Woman] Wow.

00:36:34.760 --> 00:36:39.760
- But it was a time when
the world was changing,

00:36:39.810 --> 00:36:42.313
and we wanted to be a part of that,

00:36:43.650 --> 00:36:48.650
and people made an effort
to get through the protests,

00:36:49.890 --> 00:36:52.160
to get through the degree requirements,

00:36:52.160 --> 00:36:54.860
to get through all of the psychological

00:36:54.860 --> 00:36:55.970
and emotional changes

00:36:55.970 --> 00:36:58.760
that an undergraduate would go through,

00:36:58.760 --> 00:37:02.520
and to make friends in the process.

00:37:02.520 --> 00:37:06.140
We were very fortunate in the absence

00:37:06.140 --> 00:37:11.140
of a structure at Yale for
advising and counseling,

00:37:11.630 --> 00:37:13.270
there were people in the black community

00:37:13.270 --> 00:37:16.110
who stepped in to fill those roles.

00:37:16.110 --> 00:37:19.390
There were people like Roy Bryce-Laporte,

00:37:19.390 --> 00:37:24.040
who was the head of the
Afro-American Studies program.

00:37:24.040 --> 00:37:27.460
There was Ernie Osborne
and his wife Betty,

00:37:27.460 --> 00:37:32.460
who welcomed us to their
home and into his office

00:37:32.520 --> 00:37:33.810
and became counselors.

00:37:33.810 --> 00:37:38.810
There were students who
stepped up and acted as mentors

00:37:40.200 --> 00:37:43.810
and counselors for those of us

00:37:43.810 --> 00:37:46.180
who had never been in Yale before,

00:37:46.180 --> 00:37:50.813
and the people who come to
mind are Armstead Robinson,

00:37:52.250 --> 00:37:55.718
whom you heard Sam Chauncey
mention last night,

00:37:55.718 --> 00:37:57.970
(mumbling), who was the president

00:37:57.970 --> 00:38:00.253
of the Black Student Alliance at Yale.

00:38:01.730 --> 00:38:04.580
I'd add Michael Bagneris to that group,

00:38:04.580 --> 00:38:07.900
who was the first of an entire contingent

00:38:07.900 --> 00:38:11.213
of black men who came
to Yale from St. Aug.

00:38:12.854 --> 00:38:13.980
- [Anne] Jerylle, I'm
gonna need to move on

00:38:13.980 --> 00:38:15.590
to Kathy in just one second so--

00:38:15.590 --> 00:38:17.910
- And I would just add
Sylvia Boone to the list,

00:38:17.910 --> 00:38:19.570
who when she finally came,

00:38:19.570 --> 00:38:22.523
gave us a female who could be a counselor,

00:38:23.660 --> 00:38:26.780
so those were our primary methods

00:38:26.780 --> 00:38:30.130
for establishing friendships
that, in many cases,

00:38:30.130 --> 00:38:32.290
have survived the years.

00:38:32.290 --> 00:38:33.274
- Thanks so much,

00:38:33.274 --> 00:38:36.441
(audience applauding)

00:38:38.160 --> 00:38:40.570
so, Kathy, you come from
a very different world,

00:38:40.570 --> 00:38:41.660
and I'm curious.

00:38:41.660 --> 00:38:42.630
Who were your friends,

00:38:42.630 --> 00:38:43.640
and how did you meet them,

00:38:43.640 --> 00:38:44.830
and how were they like you

00:38:44.830 --> 00:38:45.980
and different than you?

00:38:46.970 --> 00:38:51.020
- Well, I came to Yale from Mount Holyoke,

00:38:51.020 --> 00:38:53.420
and one of my four roommates,

00:38:53.420 --> 00:38:55.230
there were five of us
in the suite in Calhoun.

00:38:55.230 --> 00:38:58.330
One of the four was Mary Elizabeth Cree,

00:38:58.330 --> 00:39:00.380
who also was from Nebraska

00:39:00.380 --> 00:39:02.400
and also from Mount Holyoke.

00:39:02.400 --> 00:39:04.440
There were three Nebraskans
at Mount Holyoke.

00:39:04.440 --> 00:39:09.240
Two of us were Mary and me, and we left,

00:39:09.240 --> 00:39:11.610
but she and I were
roommates then in Calhoun,

00:39:11.610 --> 00:39:16.097
and we had three other roommates who came

00:39:17.510 --> 00:39:19.800
from the East Coast,

00:39:19.800 --> 00:39:20.700
but the five of us

00:39:20.700 --> 00:39:22.540
became really quite good friends,

00:39:22.540 --> 00:39:23.750
even though we did not spend

00:39:23.750 --> 00:39:25.280
that much time together.

00:39:25.280 --> 00:39:27.240
Mary had a boyfriend in Branford.

00:39:27.240 --> 00:39:31.218
My roommate Elaine was in
the radio station (mumbling)

00:39:31.218 --> 00:39:33.360
and was always hanging out there.

00:39:33.360 --> 00:39:34.980
One of my other roommates, Polly,

00:39:34.980 --> 00:39:36.447
was a Russian Studies major

00:39:36.447 --> 00:39:39.940
and always in the carrels in Sterling,

00:39:39.940 --> 00:39:42.600
so it's sort of amazing,
but we became close,

00:39:42.600 --> 00:39:44.860
and we're actually getting
together in three weeks

00:39:44.860 --> 00:39:47.303
to celebrate our 70th birthdays together,

00:39:49.429 --> 00:39:51.070
(audience applauding)

00:39:51.070 --> 00:39:53.490
but other than my roommates,

00:39:53.490 --> 00:39:57.270
I think the Calhoun women
got along very well.

00:39:57.270 --> 00:39:59.160
Again, we didn't spend
that much time together,

00:39:59.160 --> 00:40:01.220
but we would hang out in the courtyard

00:40:01.220 --> 00:40:02.410
and people were,

00:40:02.410 --> 00:40:04.360
when it was nice weather
and see each other,

00:40:04.360 --> 00:40:07.790
but I think the main way that
I made friends with the men,

00:40:07.790 --> 00:40:11.170
I was, as Anne said, a master's aide,

00:40:11.170 --> 00:40:12.490
which in those days,

00:40:12.490 --> 00:40:14.670
before cell phones and computers,

00:40:14.670 --> 00:40:18.540
we communicated by putting
flyers under people's doors,

00:40:18.540 --> 00:40:21.420
and one of the master's
aide's job was to go up

00:40:21.420 --> 00:40:24.250
and down the entryways
and deliver the notices

00:40:24.250 --> 00:40:27.100
about the Master's Teas
or the cocktail parties

00:40:27.100 --> 00:40:29.370
or the next lecture or whatever,

00:40:29.370 --> 00:40:32.270
so I spent a lot of time
going up and down entryways,

00:40:32.270 --> 00:40:36.080
and I obviously met a
lot of the men that way

00:40:36.080 --> 00:40:37.140
by knocking on their door

00:40:37.140 --> 00:40:39.563
and handing them flyers or invitations.

00:40:41.400 --> 00:40:44.360
The other way I met people
was that I played bridge,

00:40:44.360 --> 00:40:46.030
and I don't think
anybody's mentioned that,

00:40:46.030 --> 00:40:50.660
but I played a lot of bridge
my junior year at Yale.

00:40:50.660 --> 00:40:53.490
In fact, I played so much
bridge that it's amazing

00:40:53.490 --> 00:40:57.140
that I made it through my courses.

00:40:57.140 --> 00:40:59.460
The only all nighter I ever pulled

00:40:59.460 --> 00:41:01.565
at Yale was playing bridge,

00:41:01.565 --> 00:41:03.740
(audience laughing)

00:41:03.740 --> 00:41:05.450
and I wasn't that great a bridge player,

00:41:05.450 --> 00:41:07.520
but it was very flattering to have,

00:41:07.520 --> 00:41:10.750
especially the senior men, ask
me to play bridge with them

00:41:10.750 --> 00:41:12.500
when some of them were really

00:41:12.500 --> 00:41:15.003
very, very serious and
good bridge players,

00:41:16.020 --> 00:41:17.860
so anyway, so I played a lot of bridge,

00:41:17.860 --> 00:41:19.130
and the other thing I did

00:41:19.130 --> 00:41:21.380
was I hung out in the dining hall a lot.

00:41:21.380 --> 00:41:24.800
That was, to me, one of the
best things about being at Yale,

00:41:24.800 --> 00:41:26.810
was to sit in the dining
hall with these guys

00:41:26.810 --> 00:41:28.520
who were just very different,

00:41:28.520 --> 00:41:30.830
as somebody said, incredibly different,

00:41:30.830 --> 00:41:34.130
different points of
view, very knowledgeable,

00:41:34.130 --> 00:41:36.180
very passionate, and to just sit there

00:41:36.180 --> 00:41:38.560
and be a fly on the wall
and listen to them debate.

00:41:38.560 --> 00:41:40.363
John Bolton was in Calhoun.

00:41:41.210 --> 00:41:45.230
He was part of, I know, I know,

00:41:45.230 --> 00:41:47.580
but he was the Party of the Right guy,

00:41:47.580 --> 00:41:52.580
and then he was in a suite with Ed Farrand

00:41:52.740 --> 00:41:55.800
who was a very big player

00:41:55.800 --> 00:41:57.570
in the Democratic party in Connecticut

00:41:57.570 --> 00:41:59.250
in Al Lowenstein's campaign,

00:41:59.250 --> 00:42:02.120
so you had all these points of view,

00:42:02.120 --> 00:42:03.780
and people shared the points of view

00:42:03.780 --> 00:42:06.850
without really getting
upset with each other,

00:42:06.850 --> 00:42:08.690
and it was enlightening.

00:42:08.690 --> 00:42:11.746
- Yeah, thanks so much, so friendship.

00:42:11.746 --> 00:42:14.746
(audience laughing)

00:42:15.800 --> 00:42:16.633
Can you?

00:42:16.633 --> 00:42:18.960
Where's my wandering mic?

00:42:18.960 --> 00:42:21.730
Right there, how'd you make your friends?

00:42:21.730 --> 00:42:24.853
How were they different
than you or the same?

00:42:26.860 --> 00:42:28.620
- Hi, I'm (mumbling) Buchanan.

00:42:28.620 --> 00:42:31.427
I was in the Class of '73,

00:42:31.427 --> 00:42:34.080
and my very best friend
is sitting next to me,

00:42:34.080 --> 00:42:36.150
Nancy Breckstein.

00:42:36.150 --> 00:42:39.820
We were in Vanderbilt Hall
next door to each other,

00:42:39.820 --> 00:42:43.253
and we had some very strange roommates.

00:42:44.130 --> 00:42:45.600
She did, and I did,

00:42:45.600 --> 00:42:50.600
and Nancy commented that maybe
we were all accepted to Yale

00:42:51.710 --> 00:42:53.410
because we were all so different,

00:42:53.410 --> 00:42:55.703
and I think there's something to that,

00:42:56.970 --> 00:42:59.483
so Nancy and I were very, very close.

00:43:00.400 --> 00:43:03.510
I'll just tell about one
roommate who was Nancy's,

00:43:03.510 --> 00:43:05.760
Jenny from Mississippi.

00:43:05.760 --> 00:43:09.620
She was the fourth daughter, the youngest,

00:43:09.620 --> 00:43:13.180
of the president of the
University of Mississippi,

00:43:13.180 --> 00:43:16.070
and all of her sisters
had been debutantes.

00:43:16.070 --> 00:43:19.840
They'd all gone on the
Grand Tour in Europe,

00:43:19.840 --> 00:43:22.863
and Jenny was maybe the
original hippie trippie.

00:43:23.890 --> 00:43:28.530
Her mother would send her gowns
and these elegant clothes,

00:43:28.530 --> 00:43:31.103
hoping that somehow she could be redeemed.

00:43:32.680 --> 00:43:35.880
She would pile them in
the center of the room

00:43:35.880 --> 00:43:37.910
and let them just sit there,

00:43:37.910 --> 00:43:41.700
so later on, she got married, had a baby,

00:43:41.700 --> 00:43:46.700
nursed her baby in class in
defiance of the administration,

00:43:47.130 --> 00:43:49.590
so she was quite something,

00:43:49.590 --> 00:43:52.670
but Nancy and I were very, very close.

00:43:52.670 --> 00:43:54.270
My parents couldn't afford

00:43:54.270 --> 00:43:57.790
to let me go home at Thanksgiving,

00:43:57.790 --> 00:44:01.980
and I got to know Nancy's
whole family, her parents,

00:44:01.980 --> 00:44:05.030
and her sister at Thanksgiving,

00:44:05.030 --> 00:44:08.080
and otherwise we decided to leave.

00:44:08.080 --> 00:44:09.640
We were encouraged to leave

00:44:11.510 --> 00:44:13.260
by some friends that Mayday.

00:44:13.260 --> 00:44:14.823
We went to her house,

00:44:16.850 --> 00:44:18.360
so anyway we were very, very close

00:44:18.360 --> 00:44:21.360
throughout our Yale experience.

00:44:21.360 --> 00:44:23.670
The other friendship
that I was so fortunate

00:44:23.670 --> 00:44:27.920
to have formed was with
A. Bartlett Giamatti,

00:44:27.920 --> 00:44:30.740
who later became the president of Yale.

00:44:30.740 --> 00:44:33.110
- [Anne] I'm just
noticing the yellow card,

00:44:33.110 --> 00:44:35.770
so I'm trying to include
as many voices as possible,

00:44:35.770 --> 00:44:36.603
so if you want to--

00:44:36.603 --> 00:44:37.480
- Yeah, I'll make it quick,

00:44:37.480 --> 00:44:41.400
so he took me under his wing
and encouraged me to be a part

00:44:41.400 --> 00:44:45.000
of the History of the
Arts and Letters program.

00:44:45.000 --> 00:44:46.920
He taught in that program.

00:44:46.920 --> 00:44:48.930
It was absolutely magnificent.

00:44:48.930 --> 00:44:53.110
It made my intellectual
experience at Yale exquisite,

00:44:53.110 --> 00:44:58.110
and later on he became the
commissioner of baseball.

00:44:58.240 --> 00:44:59.803
I'm a baseball freak,

00:45:01.040 --> 00:45:04.950
so it was an exquisite experience--

00:45:04.950 --> 00:45:05.946
- [Anne] Thanks so much--

00:45:05.946 --> 00:45:07.696
- Being able to call him a friend--

00:45:09.330 --> 00:45:10.810
- And I would love it if anyone could talk

00:45:10.810 --> 00:45:12.520
about guys as friends,

00:45:12.520 --> 00:45:16.863
so 'cause that's something
I heard an awful lot about.

00:45:17.881 --> 00:45:20.227
In the back there with the,

00:45:25.372 --> 00:45:26.880
you got it?

00:45:26.880 --> 00:45:30.490
- Joyce Major, Class
of '73, Morse College.

00:45:30.490 --> 00:45:33.420
I met my group of guy friends

00:45:34.600 --> 00:45:39.600
because their freshman
counselor had a television,

00:45:40.670 --> 00:45:42.440
and every Sunday night,

00:45:42.440 --> 00:45:47.440
he would invite his Morse
College freshman boys

00:45:48.160 --> 00:45:49.737
to watch "Mission: Impossible,"

00:45:50.730 --> 00:45:52.910
and they used that as a ploy to get me

00:45:52.910 --> 00:45:54.640
to come and visit them.

00:45:54.640 --> 00:45:56.663
I think you came with me a few times,

00:46:00.058 --> 00:46:02.210
and I was from the Midwest.

00:46:02.210 --> 00:46:03.550
She's from Greenwich, Connecticut,

00:46:03.550 --> 00:46:05.300
and she and her dad picked
me up at the airport

00:46:05.300 --> 00:46:07.780
my very first time on a plane,

00:46:07.780 --> 00:46:11.750
got off at JFK having no
idea what she looked like

00:46:11.750 --> 00:46:13.350
or anything else,

00:46:13.350 --> 00:46:15.850
but the guy friends became,

00:46:15.850 --> 00:46:17.960
some of them were pre-med like I was,

00:46:17.960 --> 00:46:20.340
and we had shared some classes,

00:46:20.340 --> 00:46:25.070
but every Sunday night,
popcorn and Mission: Impossible

00:46:25.070 --> 00:46:27.010
in Harold (mumbling) room,

00:46:29.780 --> 00:46:31.520
but it was a great friendship.

00:46:31.520 --> 00:46:32.740
We lasted.

00:46:32.740 --> 00:46:35.560
We sat around the table talking.

00:46:35.560 --> 00:46:37.840
I don't think we had
political discussions,

00:46:37.840 --> 00:46:40.160
but we had a lot of
other, well, maybe we did.

00:46:40.160 --> 00:46:41.970
Did we talk politics?

00:46:41.970 --> 00:46:42.803
I don't think so.

00:46:42.803 --> 00:46:45.180
We just talked all evening.

00:46:45.180 --> 00:46:46.510
I drank so much tea,

00:46:46.510 --> 00:46:48.160
the enamel was starting to wear off,

00:46:48.160 --> 00:46:53.160
but the guys were just a very
important part of our lives,

00:46:53.380 --> 00:46:58.380
and I know that a lot of
people had guy friends that

00:46:58.730 --> 00:47:00.490
because they were dating somebody,

00:47:00.490 --> 00:47:02.880
and then the guy friend's friends

00:47:02.880 --> 00:47:04.730
became their friends, but--

00:47:04.730 --> 00:47:06.420
- Thanks so much,

00:47:06.420 --> 00:47:08.350
so we're gonna move on
to a different topic.

00:47:08.350 --> 00:47:09.183
I'm sorry.

00:47:09.183 --> 00:47:10.110
I see those hands,

00:47:10.110 --> 00:47:12.550
and maybe we'll have time
at the end to come back,

00:47:12.550 --> 00:47:16.440
but I hope you continue the
conversation afterwards.

00:47:16.440 --> 00:47:17.900
A topic that can be challenging for women,

00:47:17.900 --> 00:47:20.400
ambition, ambition,

00:47:20.400 --> 00:47:25.330
so, Kathy, I'll start
with you again or here.

00:47:25.330 --> 00:47:28.450
When you got to Yale, what
did you hope to become

00:47:28.450 --> 00:47:29.760
when you left here?

00:47:29.760 --> 00:47:32.950
And I really am still
focused on those four years

00:47:32.950 --> 00:47:36.400
and how your sense of who you
could be might have changed,

00:47:36.400 --> 00:47:37.960
and actually you were
only here for two years,

00:47:37.960 --> 00:47:40.370
so that makes it even a
little more challenging,

00:47:40.370 --> 00:47:42.760
but did your vision of what you could be

00:47:42.760 --> 00:47:43.930
or who you would be

00:47:43.930 --> 00:47:46.520
change while you were at Yale?

00:47:46.520 --> 00:47:48.730
- It didn't change so
much while I was at Yale.

00:47:48.730 --> 00:47:51.430
It changed from the time
I entered Mount Holyoke

00:47:51.430 --> 00:47:54.420
when I thought I wanted to
be in the foreign service

00:47:54.420 --> 00:47:58.550
or some kind of job where I traveled,

00:47:58.550 --> 00:48:00.360
but I took French my first year.

00:48:00.360 --> 00:48:01.670
I had taken Latin in high school.

00:48:01.670 --> 00:48:03.930
I took French my first
year at Mount Holyoke,

00:48:03.930 --> 00:48:05.540
and while reading and writing was okay,

00:48:05.540 --> 00:48:08.320
I couldn't speak it because
of my Midwestern twang,

00:48:08.320 --> 00:48:11.410
just murdered the language,

00:48:11.410 --> 00:48:13.120
and I realized if I couldn't speak French,

00:48:13.120 --> 00:48:14.120
I probably wasn't going

00:48:14.120 --> 00:48:16.420
to have an international career.

00:48:16.420 --> 00:48:18.810
So by the time I got to Yale,

00:48:18.810 --> 00:48:20.920
I was pretty set on going to law school,

00:48:20.920 --> 00:48:23.720
and pretty set, I was a
Study of the City major,

00:48:23.720 --> 00:48:28.340
and I think that the Summer of '68,

00:48:28.340 --> 00:48:30.280
after Martin Luther King was assassinated

00:48:30.280 --> 00:48:32.070
and Bobby Kennedy was assassinated,

00:48:32.070 --> 00:48:33.950
and we had the riots of '68,

00:48:33.950 --> 00:48:38.320
I became much more focused on injustice

00:48:38.320 --> 00:48:43.290
and equity and issues that
I'd not really focused

00:48:43.290 --> 00:48:45.030
on at all before then,

00:48:45.030 --> 00:48:48.070
and I decided that I really
wanted to do something

00:48:48.070 --> 00:48:50.770
in an urban context,

00:48:50.770 --> 00:48:53.950
and I thought law school
was the way to go.

00:48:53.950 --> 00:48:56.612
By the time I finished at Yale,

00:48:56.612 --> 00:49:01.322
I had focused my Study
of the City concentration

00:49:01.322 --> 00:49:03.650
on urban education,

00:49:03.650 --> 00:49:05.440
and I had spent two summers

00:49:05.440 --> 00:49:07.280
working in the South Bronx

00:49:07.280 --> 00:49:10.100
for the City of New
York's Board of Education

00:49:10.100 --> 00:49:14.100
doing title federal grant applications

00:49:14.100 --> 00:49:15.923
and things like that,

00:49:16.770 --> 00:49:18.350
and so I went to law school.

00:49:18.350 --> 00:49:19.390
I did go to law school,

00:49:19.390 --> 00:49:22.630
and I did want to participate

00:49:22.630 --> 00:49:24.610
in all the stuff that was going on

00:49:24.610 --> 00:49:26.320
in education at that point.

00:49:26.320 --> 00:49:29.690
There was busing was relatively new.

00:49:29.690 --> 00:49:31.470
The schools were still being integrated.

00:49:31.470 --> 00:49:33.010
They were trying to figure out how

00:49:33.010 --> 00:49:36.130
to provide equal educational opportunity,

00:49:36.130 --> 00:49:40.370
but I knew by the time I
was a junior that I wanted

00:49:40.370 --> 00:49:43.370
at least be a lawyer and do something in

00:49:44.310 --> 00:49:46.302
to save the world, I guess.

00:49:46.302 --> 00:49:47.272
(audience laughing)

00:49:47.272 --> 00:49:49.355
- Billie, what about you?

00:49:51.190 --> 00:49:53.120
- Ambition is such a tricky word,

00:49:53.120 --> 00:49:56.960
so and I see students a lot,

00:49:56.960 --> 00:50:01.240
and I think ambition is
much more visible now

00:50:01.240 --> 00:50:03.253
than it was when we were in school,

00:50:05.440 --> 00:50:10.343
and how to be ambitious
in a kind of world of art

00:50:12.530 --> 00:50:15.160
for me later became architecture.

00:50:15.160 --> 00:50:16.310
It's a really big question.

00:50:16.310 --> 00:50:19.870
I mean, I understood how to be ambitious

00:50:19.870 --> 00:50:23.570
as a engineer or as a scientist.

00:50:23.570 --> 00:50:25.360
I didn't actually understand how

00:50:25.360 --> 00:50:28.330
to be ambitious within the arts.

00:50:28.330 --> 00:50:32.190
I know my father, when
he saw a photograph of me

00:50:32.190 --> 00:50:36.150
that was published in the town newspaper

00:50:36.150 --> 00:50:38.740
because I was at Yale,
and in the first class,

00:50:38.740 --> 00:50:41.530
and I was barefoot, didn't
speak to me for three months,

00:50:41.530 --> 00:50:46.530
so that was definitely trying
to figure out who I could be,

00:50:48.010 --> 00:50:49.423
how I could be ambitious.

00:50:51.370 --> 00:50:55.340
The thing that I saw at Yale
was that people were ambitious

00:50:55.340 --> 00:50:58.230
in very quiet and passionate ways,

00:50:58.230 --> 00:51:01.110
so it wasn't a kind of bombastic ambition.

00:51:01.110 --> 00:51:03.230
It was an ambition and a focus

00:51:04.090 --> 00:51:06.310
on particular issues or subjects,

00:51:06.310 --> 00:51:08.467
and that was really interesting to me.

00:51:08.467 --> 00:51:10.380
The other thing that happened was

00:51:10.380 --> 00:51:14.710
I had an amazing art
teacher who has a show now

00:51:14.710 --> 00:51:15.870
at the Yale Art Gallery.

00:51:15.870 --> 00:51:17.960
His name is William Bailey,

00:51:17.960 --> 00:51:20.240
and I remember in a drawing class

00:51:20.240 --> 00:51:24.467
he said to me, "It's really not the lines.

00:51:24.467 --> 00:51:27.157
"It's the space in between the lines,"

00:51:28.160 --> 00:51:33.160
and so what that said to
me is that you don't have

00:51:33.300 --> 00:51:37.950
to be ambitious within the lines.

00:51:37.950 --> 00:51:39.843
You can find that space in between,

00:51:41.560 --> 00:51:46.175
and the desire for me
then was not to go big,

00:51:46.175 --> 00:51:47.270
but to go deep,

00:51:47.270 --> 00:51:49.618
and I think I learned that here at Yale.

00:51:49.618 --> 00:51:50.451
(audience applauding)

00:51:50.451 --> 00:51:51.589
- Thank you.

00:51:51.589 --> 00:51:53.839
(mumbling)

00:51:57.460 --> 00:51:59.920
Now to you, ambition?

00:51:59.920 --> 00:52:01.457
Yes, right there.

00:52:03.812 --> 00:52:06.062
(mumbling)

00:52:06.990 --> 00:52:08.430
- I'm going to hand it to Mally,

00:52:08.430 --> 00:52:11.130
but just real quick, Casey Blanchard.

00:52:11.130 --> 00:52:13.410
Good morning, Carol.

00:52:13.410 --> 00:52:15.460
My roommate, I'm over here,

00:52:15.460 --> 00:52:18.476
and we were talking, going to Yale,

00:52:18.476 --> 00:52:20.780
I just felt I could do anything,

00:52:20.780 --> 00:52:22.440
and that was always just easy.

00:52:22.440 --> 00:52:23.720
After you got through Yale,

00:52:23.720 --> 00:52:25.687
like living in New York,
you can do anything--

00:52:25.687 --> 00:52:26.604
- Anything.

00:52:27.620 --> 00:52:30.410
I chose Yale over
Harvard because I thought

00:52:30.410 --> 00:52:32.370
there was a better chance that I could get

00:52:32.370 --> 00:52:33.860
on the Yale Daily News--

00:52:33.860 --> 00:52:36.310
- [Anne] Can you tell us
just your name and your year?

00:52:36.310 --> 00:52:38.363
- Mally Cox Chapman, '73,

00:52:39.320 --> 00:52:41.290
but the first year when we were supposed

00:52:41.290 --> 00:52:43.030
to (mumbling) was overwhelming.

00:52:43.030 --> 00:52:44.340
I didn't do it,

00:52:44.340 --> 00:52:46.820
so sophomore year I went
into the news office

00:52:46.820 --> 00:52:49.440
with five articles on women at Yale,

00:52:49.440 --> 00:52:50.850
a two year perspective,

00:52:50.850 --> 00:52:54.345
and said I'd like these all to
be published on the same day,

00:52:54.345 --> 00:52:56.160
(audience laughing)

00:52:56.160 --> 00:52:57.330
and they did it,

00:52:57.330 --> 00:53:00.790
and I was immediately
allowed to be a reporter.

00:53:00.790 --> 00:53:03.260
Well, it came time to have the elections,

00:53:03.260 --> 00:53:05.430
and I had this tape in my head

00:53:05.430 --> 00:53:07.360
from my girls' boarding school

00:53:07.360 --> 00:53:09.660
that you don't show your ambition.

00:53:09.660 --> 00:53:11.100
You do your work.

00:53:11.100 --> 00:53:12.200
You do it well,

00:53:12.200 --> 00:53:14.280
and people will notice,

00:53:14.280 --> 00:53:16.350
so the week before the election,

00:53:16.350 --> 00:53:19.000
I didn't go over to the
Yale Daily News office,

00:53:19.000 --> 00:53:22.060
and everybody else was over there lobbying

00:53:22.060 --> 00:53:24.280
for the position that they wanted,

00:53:24.280 --> 00:53:26.600
so of course I didn't get elected.

00:53:26.600 --> 00:53:28.170
The election was over.

00:53:28.170 --> 00:53:29.790
People are drifting away,

00:53:29.790 --> 00:53:32.500
and the five guys who
are now on the masthead

00:53:32.500 --> 00:53:36.087
looked at me and said, "You jerk.

00:53:36.087 --> 00:53:39.037
"Why weren't you here this week?

00:53:39.037 --> 00:53:40.957
"What were you doing not being here?

00:53:40.957 --> 00:53:43.320
"What are we gonna do
without you on the team?"

00:53:43.320 --> 00:53:45.550
And I told them the tape that I had,

00:53:45.550 --> 00:53:48.477
and they said, "Name what you want to do,

00:53:48.477 --> 00:53:51.430
"and we'll just make up a position,"

00:53:51.430 --> 00:53:53.010
so they gave me the position

00:53:53.010 --> 00:53:55.350
of Chief Staff Writer Feature Editor,

00:53:55.350 --> 00:53:57.490
and I presented a feature every day

00:53:57.490 --> 00:54:00.630
for the next year on the
front page below the fold,

00:54:00.630 --> 00:54:04.410
so really it was the men, my friends,

00:54:04.410 --> 00:54:06.930
who taught me how to name what I wanted

00:54:06.930 --> 00:54:09.410
and ask for what I wanted.

00:54:09.410 --> 00:54:10.243
- [Woman] Wow.

00:54:10.243 --> 00:54:11.076
- Thank you.

00:54:11.076 --> 00:54:14.212
(audience applauding)

00:54:14.212 --> 00:54:16.462
(mumbling)

00:54:18.083 --> 00:54:20.270
Someone right here in the third row,

00:54:20.270 --> 00:54:22.423
or who has been raising their hand.

00:54:26.270 --> 00:54:28.380
- I'm Emily Fine, JE, '73.

00:54:28.380 --> 00:54:29.770
That's an amazing story,

00:54:29.770 --> 00:54:31.280
and I wish I could say that

00:54:31.280 --> 00:54:34.320
that is reflective of
a lot of other stories,

00:54:34.320 --> 00:54:35.930
but I'm not sure it is.

00:54:35.930 --> 00:54:39.520
I think the elephant
in the room for me is,

00:54:39.520 --> 00:54:42.210
and I think as women
and still to this day,

00:54:42.210 --> 00:54:45.120
is that it's not always so cool
for women to look ambitious,

00:54:45.120 --> 00:54:49.090
and we somehow have to find a
classier way to look ambitious

00:54:49.090 --> 00:54:50.430
because if you're too ambitious,

00:54:50.430 --> 00:54:54.180
that's not necessarily
considered acceptable.

00:54:54.180 --> 00:54:56.990
It's changing obviously thankfully,

00:54:56.990 --> 00:55:00.960
but we ride a sort of fine balance.

00:55:00.960 --> 00:55:02.150
That's one.

00:55:02.150 --> 00:55:05.280
Two, the times, I remember by the end

00:55:05.280 --> 00:55:06.360
of my sophomore year,

00:55:06.360 --> 00:55:09.190
if I was working on women's
abortion referral service

00:55:09.190 --> 00:55:10.980
or women's support groups,

00:55:10.980 --> 00:55:11.813
and they would say,

00:55:11.813 --> 00:55:13.420
"Oh, what college do you go to,"

00:55:13.420 --> 00:55:15.906
I would say, I'm in New Haven.

00:55:15.906 --> 00:55:16.739
(audience laughing)

00:55:16.739 --> 00:55:19.520
There was a sense of
embarrassment of being part

00:55:19.520 --> 00:55:24.520
of the elite and sort of a
hope that maybe they thought

00:55:24.890 --> 00:55:27.680
we were going to Southern
Connecticut instead,

00:55:27.680 --> 00:55:30.500
so that also complicated ambition,

00:55:30.500 --> 00:55:31.990
but I will say just one shout out,

00:55:31.990 --> 00:55:32.823
and then I'll be done,

00:55:32.823 --> 00:55:36.790
about engagement slash the other issue,

00:55:36.790 --> 00:55:38.240
which is music.

00:55:38.240 --> 00:55:43.230
So for me, I signed up
for courses right very on

00:55:43.230 --> 00:55:44.610
and within two weeks, I dropped them all.

00:55:44.610 --> 00:55:45.630
I was in sciences.

00:55:45.630 --> 00:55:48.890
I felt completely isolated,
overwhelmed, unsupported.

00:55:48.890 --> 00:55:50.310
I eventually obviously came back to it

00:55:50.310 --> 00:55:51.480
'cause I'm a physician,

00:55:51.480 --> 00:55:54.580
but I loved music, which
was my other passion,

00:55:54.580 --> 00:55:57.540
and it was at Yale Symphony
where it didn't matter

00:55:57.540 --> 00:55:58.373
what your sex was.

00:55:58.373 --> 00:56:00.850
If you could play a mean
horn, you had friends

00:56:00.850 --> 00:56:02.230
who were men and women,

00:56:02.230 --> 00:56:05.540
and there were lots of
women in the Symphony.

00:56:05.540 --> 00:56:08.480
The ratio was much better, so for me,

00:56:08.480 --> 00:56:10.930
my sense of security and home,

00:56:10.930 --> 00:56:12.050
and because I didn't feel

00:56:12.050 --> 00:56:13.710
I had the support elsewhere,

00:56:13.710 --> 00:56:15.380
was in my music life at Yale,

00:56:15.380 --> 00:56:17.270
for which I'll be forever grateful.

00:56:17.270 --> 00:56:18.471
- Thank you so much.

00:56:18.471 --> 00:56:21.638
(audience applauding)

00:56:25.260 --> 00:56:26.460
I apologize to the panel.

00:56:26.460 --> 00:56:28.180
I'm skipping around the
order a little bit here,

00:56:28.180 --> 00:56:29.013
but I'm gonna (mumbling).

00:56:29.013 --> 00:56:29.846
I haven't forgotten you,

00:56:29.846 --> 00:56:34.363
so we're turning to
romance and relationships.

00:56:36.161 --> 00:56:37.461
The Yale women (laughing),

00:56:39.445 --> 00:56:40.278
(mumbling)

00:56:40.278 --> 00:56:42.134
but let me ask the question first.

00:56:42.134 --> 00:56:43.960
(mumbling)

00:56:43.960 --> 00:56:47.010
Yale women adopted a variety of approaches

00:56:47.010 --> 00:56:51.290
when it came to romantic
relationships with Yale men.

00:56:51.290 --> 00:56:53.510
Some dated multiple guys.

00:56:53.510 --> 00:56:55.750
Some had one steady boyfriend.

00:56:55.750 --> 00:56:58.610
Some wanted a boyfriend,
but didn't have one.

00:56:58.610 --> 00:57:01.870
Some chose not to have
a Yale boyfriend at all,

00:57:01.870 --> 00:57:04.903
and some dated, or wanted to date, women.

00:57:05.830 --> 00:57:07.980
What approach did you take,

00:57:07.980 --> 00:57:10.015
and how did that work out for you?

00:57:10.015 --> 00:57:11.470
(audience laughing)

00:57:11.470 --> 00:57:14.363
So, Joan, I'm gonna give that to you.

00:57:15.360 --> 00:57:16.204
- Me?

00:57:16.204 --> 00:57:17.037
(laughing) Well--

00:57:17.037 --> 00:57:18.220
- Is that all right?

00:57:18.220 --> 00:57:19.550
- Yeah, no, no, no, it's fine.

00:57:19.550 --> 00:57:21.360
I'm just I thought I'd tell you a story

00:57:21.360 --> 00:57:22.683
I've never told anybody.

00:57:24.610 --> 00:57:29.210
I fell in love my first day at Yale.

00:57:29.210 --> 00:57:32.400
When I went from Vanderbilt
Hall over to Davenport

00:57:32.400 --> 00:57:35.150
to the Commons Room for the welcome thing,

00:57:35.150 --> 00:57:37.400
I saw this guy across the room

00:57:37.400 --> 00:57:39.600
who I thought was amazing.

00:57:39.600 --> 00:57:41.090
I don't know what it was,

00:57:41.090 --> 00:57:44.190
and I thought our eyes
met, but I wasn't sure.

00:57:44.190 --> 00:57:46.450
So I don't know if you guys remember,

00:57:46.450 --> 00:57:48.890
but we'd go back to our rooms most

00:57:48.890 --> 00:57:50.760
of the days in the first weeks,

00:57:50.760 --> 00:57:53.430
and there'd be a little
note pinned to the door

00:57:53.430 --> 00:57:55.040
of people who wanted
to meet us or something

00:57:55.040 --> 00:57:56.930
'cause I think the guys
felt if they didn't get

00:57:56.930 --> 00:58:00.830
a girlfriend like really
fast, it was over,

00:58:00.830 --> 00:58:03.530
so there was a note on my
door, but it wasn't signed.

00:58:03.530 --> 00:58:05.640
It was signed with initials,

00:58:05.640 --> 00:58:07.560
and so of course I thought,

00:58:07.560 --> 00:58:09.497
maybe it's him,

00:58:09.497 --> 00:58:10.810
but I didn't know his name,

00:58:10.810 --> 00:58:12.827
and I looked through the Facebook thing,

00:58:12.827 --> 00:58:14.350
and he wasn't in there

00:58:14.350 --> 00:58:15.183
'cause I don't know.

00:58:15.183 --> 00:58:16.980
He didn't send in a picture or something.

00:58:16.980 --> 00:58:20.060
Any rate, it turned out it was from him,

00:58:20.060 --> 00:58:23.970
and I was just like on cloud nine,

00:58:23.970 --> 00:58:26.590
and we got together,

00:58:26.590 --> 00:58:30.170
and I moved out of Vanderbilt

00:58:30.170 --> 00:58:35.170
and into his dorm on the old
campus very quickly actually,

00:58:35.290 --> 00:58:37.370
and, I mean, I met my roommates,

00:58:37.370 --> 00:58:38.670
but I didn't really know anyone

00:58:38.670 --> 00:58:39.569
in the entryway,

00:58:39.569 --> 00:58:40.848
(audience laughing)

00:58:40.848 --> 00:58:43.330
so guess what happened?

00:58:43.330 --> 00:58:48.130
Parents Day, so my parents are
traveling from San Franciso

00:58:48.130 --> 00:58:50.550
for the first time to see me at Yale,

00:58:50.550 --> 00:58:52.610
and they want to meet my roommates,

00:58:52.610 --> 00:58:55.930
so I had to go over before Parents Day,

00:58:55.930 --> 00:58:58.300
and I knocked on every
door in the entryway,

00:58:58.300 --> 00:58:59.920
but I wasn't delivering flyers.

00:58:59.920 --> 00:59:03.140
I was trying to meet the people and said,

00:59:03.140 --> 00:59:05.563
please just pretend you know me.

00:59:05.563 --> 00:59:08.563
(audience laughing)

00:59:10.601 --> 00:59:14.018
So I had very few friendships with women,

00:59:14.860 --> 00:59:17.530
but I had a lot of brothers,

00:59:17.530 --> 00:59:22.530
and it so happened that my boyfriend's,

00:59:23.200 --> 00:59:25.630
one of his roommates was a bridge fanatic,

00:59:25.630 --> 00:59:27.130
and I know that bridge scene

00:59:27.130 --> 00:59:30.760
because they were there all
the time playing all night.

00:59:30.760 --> 00:59:32.480
I mean, it was a thing.

00:59:32.480 --> 00:59:36.107
Any rate, so that happened,

00:59:36.107 --> 00:59:41.107
but I want to talk about the Sarrels.

00:59:42.950 --> 00:59:44.812
No one's mentioned them,

00:59:44.812 --> 00:59:47.110
(audience applauding)

00:59:47.110 --> 00:59:48.179
but for me--

00:59:48.179 --> 00:59:50.240
- So you all can hear,
Phil and Lorna Sarrel--

00:59:50.240 --> 00:59:52.440
- I just have to tell just a little story.

00:59:52.440 --> 00:59:53.780
I think all of us,

00:59:53.780 --> 00:59:56.050
but I know I got a
phone call or something.

00:59:56.050 --> 00:59:58.207
They contacted me, and they said,

00:59:58.207 --> 01:00:01.657
"Please make an appointment
to see Dr. Sarrel,

01:00:01.657 --> 01:00:02.490
"Dr. and Mrs. Sarrel,"

01:00:02.490 --> 01:00:04.130
and I'm like, why?

01:00:04.130 --> 01:00:07.050
Oh, well, they're the gynecologist,

01:00:07.050 --> 01:00:09.210
and his wife is a social
worker or something.

01:00:09.210 --> 01:00:10.960
I (mumbling) sure,

01:00:10.960 --> 01:00:14.380
so they want to meet every
woman in the first class,

01:00:14.380 --> 01:00:15.600
so I'm like, okay.

01:00:15.600 --> 01:00:16.900
So I go over there,

01:00:16.900 --> 01:00:21.900
and this was like a interview
rather than an exam,

01:00:22.210 --> 01:00:25.650
and they said, "Have you ever had sex?"

01:00:25.650 --> 01:00:27.634
And I said, no,

01:00:27.634 --> 01:00:28.840
(audience laughing)

01:00:28.840 --> 01:00:29.987
and they said,

01:00:29.987 --> 01:00:33.067
"We would like to prescribe
you birth control.

01:00:33.067 --> 01:00:36.257
"Would you like an IUD or
would you prefer the pill?"

01:00:37.150 --> 01:00:39.680
And I said, why do I need birth control

01:00:39.680 --> 01:00:41.330
if I haven't had sex?

01:00:41.330 --> 01:00:45.827
And they said, "Kingman
Brewster said no pregnancies,"

01:00:48.750 --> 01:00:52.940
so I got a prescription for the pill,

01:00:52.940 --> 01:00:55.550
and I thought, well, maybe
someday I'll take it,

01:00:55.550 --> 01:00:57.350
and it did come in handy eventually,

01:00:58.478 --> 01:01:03.478
but what I want to say about
friendships is ironically,

01:01:03.850 --> 01:01:05.467
the way I got to know more women,

01:01:05.467 --> 01:01:07.290
and this was the second year.

01:01:07.290 --> 01:01:08.670
I was in Davenport now--

01:01:08.670 --> 01:01:09.584
- [Anne] All right, Joan--

01:01:09.584 --> 01:01:10.417
- It's almost over,

01:01:10.417 --> 01:01:11.873
but you'll be glad to hear this.

01:01:11.873 --> 01:01:14.360
(audience laughing)

01:01:14.360 --> 01:01:19.360
There was a survey for sophomores where,

01:01:19.720 --> 01:01:22.050
I don't know if you guys remember this,

01:01:22.050 --> 01:01:23.790
but I'll never forget it,

01:01:23.790 --> 01:01:27.370
where they circulated a printed survey.

01:01:27.370 --> 01:01:31.170
It was about our sexual practices,

01:01:31.170 --> 01:01:33.917
and it was long, and it said,

01:01:33.917 --> 01:01:35.630
"Have you ever,"

01:01:35.630 --> 01:01:38.370
and then it listed all these words,

01:01:38.370 --> 01:01:41.750
and I really didn't know
what some of them were,

01:01:41.750 --> 01:01:44.580
and so all of us, the girls were talking,

01:01:44.580 --> 01:01:46.970
like have you ever done this?

01:01:46.970 --> 01:01:48.560
And not only did it say, "Have you ever,"

01:01:48.560 --> 01:01:53.127
it said, "If so, how many
times a week, a month?"

01:01:54.160 --> 01:01:55.720
I mean, it was something else,

01:01:55.720 --> 01:01:57.250
so at any rate, that was like one

01:01:57.250 --> 01:02:00.083
of my real moments at Yale.

01:02:01.470 --> 01:02:02.493
- Thanks, Joan.

01:02:02.493 --> 01:02:04.180
(audience applauding)

01:02:04.180 --> 01:02:07.290
Jerylle, do you want to
speak to this as well?

01:02:07.290 --> 01:02:08.518
You can pass if you want.

01:02:08.518 --> 01:02:11.090
(laughing)

01:02:11.090 --> 01:02:12.313
- I'll be really short.

01:02:13.560 --> 01:02:18.363
I was lucky to have lots of male friends,

01:02:19.970 --> 01:02:23.123
and most of them were like brothers.

01:02:24.260 --> 01:02:28.050
I also was lucky in
finding a really great guy

01:02:28.050 --> 01:02:32.393
my second year who was
in the class behind me.

01:02:33.580 --> 01:02:37.003
The difficulty came when I graduated.

01:02:38.470 --> 01:02:42.780
The relationship worked fine
while we were on campus,

01:02:42.780 --> 01:02:45.540
fabulous guy, photographer.

01:02:45.540 --> 01:02:48.103
I even learned what to do in a darkroom.

01:02:49.122 --> 01:02:52.465
(audience laughing)

01:02:52.465 --> 01:02:53.433
I didn't mean that.

01:02:57.430 --> 01:02:58.673
He was good teacher.

01:03:00.930 --> 01:03:02.258
- [Anne] You're digging a hole here.

01:03:02.258 --> 01:03:05.130
(audience laughing)

01:03:05.130 --> 01:03:06.900
- But when we graduated,

01:03:06.900 --> 01:03:09.463
when I graduated and moved to New York,

01:03:10.760 --> 01:03:13.370
our lives took different paths,

01:03:13.370 --> 01:03:16.500
and the relationship couldn't
withstand the changes,

01:03:16.500 --> 01:03:19.140
so we went our separate ways,

01:03:19.140 --> 01:03:23.710
but I was always grateful
for having had shared

01:03:23.710 --> 01:03:26.460
this experience with him
because it made a difference.

01:03:26.460 --> 01:03:27.343
- Thanks so much.

01:03:28.880 --> 01:03:31.090
Anyone else want to speak to--

01:03:31.090 --> 01:03:32.430
(laughing)

01:03:32.430 --> 01:03:33.520
(mumbling)

01:03:33.520 --> 01:03:34.470
- She's gonna levitate.

01:03:34.470 --> 01:03:35.303
- Gee, I--

01:03:36.800 --> 01:03:38.750
- Yeah, she raised her hand (mumbling).

01:03:40.034 --> 01:03:41.388
- Yeah, Alexis, right there.

01:03:41.388 --> 01:03:43.638
(mumbling)

01:03:49.350 --> 01:03:53.763
- I'm Alexis Krasilovsky, Berkeley, '71.

01:03:54.650 --> 01:03:57.640
I came to Yale with a
great deal of passion

01:03:57.640 --> 01:04:00.920
and ambition with regards to the arts.

01:04:00.920 --> 01:04:03.400
I was so excited to be here.

01:04:03.400 --> 01:04:06.380
I was also extremely eager to participate

01:04:06.380 --> 01:04:08.383
in the sexual liberation movement.

01:04:10.160 --> 01:04:14.550
I didn't realize that there
were pros and cons to that,

01:04:14.550 --> 01:04:19.550
and I guess I suffered from
post-traumatic stress disorder

01:04:19.840 --> 01:04:22.500
from some of the sexual encounters.

01:04:22.500 --> 01:04:26.110
Of course PTSD wasn't a
term that existed back then.

01:04:27.090 --> 01:04:30.590
It took me 20 years to write a novel

01:04:32.130 --> 01:04:34.840
that was in part a fictionalization

01:04:34.840 --> 01:04:36.920
of some of those experiences,

01:04:36.920 --> 01:04:38.330
and part one of the novel

01:04:38.330 --> 01:04:41.578
is called "Pillow Book of a Yale Coed."

01:04:41.578 --> 01:04:44.130
I have postcards here to pass out

01:04:44.130 --> 01:04:46.450
so that some of you will read the book.

01:04:46.450 --> 01:04:48.360
I hope you'll let me know

01:04:48.360 --> 01:04:49.410
what you think about it.

01:04:49.410 --> 01:04:54.410
It won an award for best Me Too
novel of the year last year.

01:04:54.612 --> 01:04:58.560
It definitely has a
lot of erotic passages.

01:04:58.560 --> 01:05:00.500
I've had to publish it under a pseudonym

01:05:00.500 --> 01:05:02.590
because my dad was alive.

01:05:02.590 --> 01:05:04.833
I thought he'd be horrified and disown me,

01:05:06.130 --> 01:05:07.270
but I hope you read the book

01:05:07.270 --> 01:05:08.550
and let me know what you think.

01:05:08.550 --> 01:05:09.430
Thank you.

01:05:09.430 --> 01:05:10.698
- Thanks, Alexis.

01:05:10.698 --> 01:05:13.040
(audience applauding)

01:05:13.040 --> 01:05:15.760
Way, way, way in the very back row.

01:05:15.760 --> 01:05:16.771
- [Alexis] Should I give it to somebody--

01:05:16.771 --> 01:05:17.770
- Can we get a mic back there?

01:05:17.770 --> 01:05:18.830
Oh, I'm sorry.

01:05:18.830 --> 01:05:21.400
Okay, we'll do you and
then back row, okay?

01:05:21.400 --> 01:05:22.950
- Well, I'll try to make it short.

01:05:22.950 --> 01:05:25.350
I'm Marcia Eckert, '71,

01:05:25.350 --> 01:05:28.423
so I'm one of those people
who came in and floated.

01:05:29.280 --> 01:05:31.120
Everybody else is
passionate and had friends,

01:05:31.120 --> 01:05:32.820
and I had a double.

01:05:32.820 --> 01:05:35.340
My roommate was a white Black Panther,

01:05:35.340 --> 01:05:37.690
so she kind of took off,

01:05:37.690 --> 01:05:40.440
and I was wandering
around the first semester

01:05:40.440 --> 01:05:42.150
as part of a team

01:05:42.150 --> 01:05:46.360
from TD interviewing
people about coeducation,

01:05:46.360 --> 01:05:49.530
and so I was sort of on my own,

01:05:49.530 --> 01:05:51.683
I didn't know a lot of people,

01:05:52.575 --> 01:05:56.840
and when the Black Panther
office got busted in New Haven,

01:05:56.840 --> 01:05:59.110
they moved into my dorm room.

01:05:59.110 --> 01:06:02.440
So there were people going,
"Power to the people,"

01:06:02.440 --> 01:06:05.030
and, I mean, I was really interested,

01:06:05.030 --> 01:06:07.820
but it was all part of my
sort of floating around,

01:06:07.820 --> 01:06:10.190
so when Mayday came,

01:06:10.190 --> 01:06:13.160
and I think my room was closed

01:06:13.160 --> 01:06:15.290
because it had been this office.

01:06:15.290 --> 01:06:16.758
I didn't know what the heck to do.

01:06:16.758 --> 01:06:18.450
I mean, I hung out with guys,

01:06:18.450 --> 01:06:19.810
but I wasn't close friends with them.

01:06:19.810 --> 01:06:20.930
I didn't really know any women,

01:06:20.930 --> 01:06:23.910
and I sure as hell wasn't
going home to my mother,

01:06:23.910 --> 01:06:27.480
so I had started just six
weeks before dating someone

01:06:27.480 --> 01:06:29.740
in the theater in the Yale Rep

01:06:29.740 --> 01:06:32.840
who had come over to me in the mail room

01:06:32.840 --> 01:06:34.860
and said, "I saw this light in your eyes,"

01:06:34.860 --> 01:06:36.940
and I thought it was a
social psych experiment

01:06:36.940 --> 01:06:37.902
on pickup lines,

01:06:37.902 --> 01:06:41.300
(audience laughing)

01:06:41.300 --> 01:06:43.960
and he said, "Well, you
could stay in my apartment,"

01:06:43.960 --> 01:06:45.720
and I said, well, my mother's very strict.

01:06:45.720 --> 01:06:48.360
I could never stay in an
apartment unless I was married.

01:06:48.360 --> 01:06:49.980
He said, "Well, then let's get married,"

01:06:49.980 --> 01:06:51.179
and I said, okay,

01:06:51.179 --> 01:06:54.310
(audience laughing)

01:06:54.310 --> 01:06:56.965
(mumbling)

01:06:56.965 --> 01:06:57.798
so I did,

01:07:01.230 --> 01:07:02.540
(mumbling)

01:07:02.540 --> 01:07:04.640
so that's how I ended up with a boyfriend.

01:07:06.480 --> 01:07:07.456
- Wow.

01:07:07.456 --> 01:07:08.940
(audience laughing)

01:07:08.940 --> 01:07:09.947
- Are you (mumbling)?

01:07:12.552 --> 01:07:14.286
We need the end of that story.

01:07:14.286 --> 01:07:17.884
(audience laughing)

01:07:17.884 --> 01:07:19.124
- [Woman] What happened?

01:07:19.124 --> 01:07:20.648
- [Woman] What happened?

01:07:20.648 --> 01:07:21.481
(mumbling)

01:07:21.481 --> 01:07:23.314
- Yeah, what happened?

01:07:24.520 --> 01:07:25.353
- Wait, wait, wait.

01:07:25.353 --> 01:07:27.670
Can you use the mic so that
everyone can hear (mumbling)?

01:07:29.140 --> 01:07:30.040
- For those who want to know,

01:07:30.040 --> 01:07:35.000
it was kind of like going
steady except legally,

01:07:35.000 --> 01:07:37.130
and so we were together

01:07:37.130 --> 01:07:39.440
for about three really fascinating years,

01:07:39.440 --> 01:07:41.420
and I got to meet a lot
of interesting people

01:07:41.420 --> 01:07:43.450
who were in the Yale Rep at the time,

01:07:43.450 --> 01:07:45.610
like Christopher Walken
and Christopher Lloyd,

01:07:45.610 --> 01:07:48.500
and Meryl Streep was in the drama school

01:07:48.500 --> 01:07:50.200
and Henry Winkler was sort of,

01:07:50.200 --> 01:07:51.341
we all lived together,

01:07:51.341 --> 01:07:54.440
so it was a really interesting, weird time

01:07:54.440 --> 01:07:59.410
being an undergraduate and a
theater wife simultaneously,

01:07:59.410 --> 01:08:02.560
but after about three
years, we drifted apart,

01:08:02.560 --> 01:08:05.046
and I went back to being sort of normal.

01:08:05.046 --> 01:08:06.907
(audience laughing)

01:08:06.907 --> 01:08:08.569
- [Woman] Back to being single.

01:08:08.569 --> 01:08:09.782
- In the way back there.

01:08:09.782 --> 01:08:12.032
(mumbling)

01:08:14.420 --> 01:08:17.663
- I'm Liz Oliver, Davenport, '71,

01:08:19.520 --> 01:08:22.420
two parts, a little bit
about dealing with ambition

01:08:22.420 --> 01:08:25.003
and also relationship to guy friends.

01:08:26.030 --> 01:08:27.700
Early on, I don't know.

01:08:27.700 --> 01:08:29.070
There must be other people here

01:08:29.070 --> 01:08:32.660
who got asked to go to
alumni dinners to talk

01:08:32.660 --> 01:08:36.550
or sit next to at the
various faculty homes

01:08:36.550 --> 01:08:39.170
old crusty alums who were really upset

01:08:39.170 --> 01:08:40.960
that Yale had gone coed.

01:08:40.960 --> 01:08:41.793
I don't know.

01:08:41.793 --> 01:08:43.070
Is there anybody else who
went to those dinners?

01:08:43.070 --> 01:08:44.510
Yay, okay,

01:08:44.510 --> 01:08:48.560
so at one of these
dinners, and I have no idea

01:08:48.560 --> 01:08:50.270
how they selected who they selected,

01:08:50.270 --> 01:08:51.500
but at one these dinners,

01:08:51.500 --> 01:08:53.730
I sat next to someone who had graduated,

01:08:53.730 --> 01:08:55.240
I believe if memory serves,

01:08:55.240 --> 01:08:57.430
and that's questionable at this point,

01:08:57.430 --> 01:09:00.203
but I think it was 1903 or 1906,

01:09:01.470 --> 01:09:03.940
and he was livid,

01:09:03.940 --> 01:09:07.490
and I had been at Smith for two years,

01:09:07.490 --> 01:09:08.410
and one of the reasons

01:09:08.410 --> 01:09:09.690
I wanted to leave Smith

01:09:09.690 --> 01:09:11.940
was I grew up in New York.

01:09:11.940 --> 01:09:14.650
I went to a huge public
high school with 5,000 kids

01:09:14.650 --> 01:09:16.700
and was so used to--

01:09:16.700 --> 01:09:17.763
- [Woman] Use your mic.

01:09:18.611 --> 01:09:19.600
(mumbling)

01:09:19.600 --> 01:09:24.600
- Sorry, and Smith felt
very unnatural to me,

01:09:26.010 --> 01:09:26.843
being an all girls school,

01:09:26.843 --> 01:09:28.960
so I was looking forward
to coeducation at Yale.

01:09:28.960 --> 01:09:32.170
However, this guy was telling
me how unwelcome I was

01:09:32.170 --> 01:09:36.620
and assuming that essentially
I was taking the place,

01:09:36.620 --> 01:09:38.767
which he said to me flat out,

01:09:38.767 --> 01:09:40.233
"What did I want to do with my life?"

01:09:40.233 --> 01:09:43.410
All I wanted to do was get
married and find a husband,

01:09:43.410 --> 01:09:45.910
and that was my ambition to come to Yale,

01:09:45.910 --> 01:09:49.300
and I was taking the place of one

01:09:49.300 --> 01:09:51.710
of Yale's 1,000 male leaders,

01:09:51.710 --> 01:09:54.650
so I remember leaving that
dinner and thinking to myself,

01:09:54.650 --> 01:09:58.330
that this was a very strange perspective

01:09:58.330 --> 01:10:03.330
to plant on first year girls
wandering around the campus.

01:10:04.160 --> 01:10:09.090
Fast forward, after
having many male friends

01:10:09.090 --> 01:10:12.260
in the college, someone came up to me

01:10:13.160 --> 01:10:15.880
with another classmate
who had been drafted

01:10:15.880 --> 01:10:17.430
whose number had come up,

01:10:17.430 --> 01:10:19.200
and he had dropped out for while,

01:10:19.200 --> 01:10:20.033
and they got him,

01:10:20.033 --> 01:10:21.390
and he was about to be drafted,

01:10:21.390 --> 01:10:24.240
and the thing that he
was most worried about

01:10:24.240 --> 01:10:27.030
was that they were going to cut his hair,

01:10:27.030 --> 01:10:29.140
that his hair, which he had grown,

01:10:29.140 --> 01:10:31.340
I mean, he was worried
about a lot of things,

01:10:31.340 --> 01:10:33.740
but it was an obsession.

01:10:33.740 --> 01:10:38.740
And so somebody named Gus
was going from table to table

01:10:38.940 --> 01:10:41.580
asking if anybody knew how to cut hair,

01:10:41.580 --> 01:10:44.670
which for whatever
reasons, I happened to do,

01:10:44.670 --> 01:10:48.000
so we went to essentially--

01:10:48.000 --> 01:10:49.990
- [Anne] I'm just observing
you got your yellow card,

01:10:49.990 --> 01:10:52.538
so if you can finish
this story so we can then

01:10:52.538 --> 01:10:53.371
let someone else talk--

01:10:53.371 --> 01:10:55.380
- Okay, very quickly I cut this guy's hair

01:10:55.380 --> 01:10:57.230
as he got himself drunker and drunker

01:10:57.230 --> 01:11:00.250
preparing to go into,
report to basic training,

01:11:00.250 --> 01:11:01.640
or to report to the local,

01:11:01.640 --> 01:11:05.470
and the guy who brought him
over became a close friend,

01:11:05.470 --> 01:11:07.970
and then ultimately became my husband

01:11:07.970 --> 01:11:10.610
with whom I am married now

01:11:10.610 --> 01:11:14.640
for going on 47, six years.

01:11:14.640 --> 01:11:16.610
I would like to very much to find the guy

01:11:16.610 --> 01:11:19.430
from 1903 to tell him in
fact I did find a husband.

01:11:19.430 --> 01:11:22.240
(audience laughing)

01:11:22.240 --> 01:11:23.143
- [Woman] Thank you.

01:11:29.094 --> 01:11:32.017
- I will, but I'd rather
speak on feminism.

01:11:32.017 --> 01:11:32.850
- Okay.

01:11:35.600 --> 01:11:38.773
We have so many questions
and so little time.

01:11:40.580 --> 01:11:43.090
The question I'm gonna
talk about, ask next,

01:11:43.090 --> 01:11:46.530
is on feminism 'cause
you all arrived really

01:11:46.530 --> 01:11:50.143
just when the women's movement
is starting to get going,

01:11:51.560 --> 01:11:55.340
and so, Cai, how did your
understanding of feminism change

01:11:55.340 --> 01:11:56.700
over your time at Yale,

01:11:56.700 --> 01:11:58.850
and I'm wondering if the
women's movement was something

01:11:58.850 --> 01:11:59.950
that was important to you,

01:11:59.950 --> 01:12:00.840
either when you got here

01:12:00.840 --> 01:12:02.870
or by the time you left?

01:12:02.870 --> 01:12:05.150
- Okay, at first, I just want to say

01:12:05.150 --> 01:12:06.967
that Anne just asked me,

01:12:06.967 --> 01:12:10.330
"Would I be willing to speak
about sexual harassment?"

01:12:10.330 --> 01:12:13.300
And I said, I would, but I'd
rather speak about feminism,

01:12:13.300 --> 01:12:17.670
so but can I just say something briefly

01:12:17.670 --> 01:12:19.023
about sexual harassment?

01:12:21.087 --> 01:12:23.190
Now that I've said I don't
want to talk about it.

01:12:23.190 --> 01:12:26.730
I was not actually harassed by any people

01:12:26.730 --> 01:12:28.300
in authority while I was here,

01:12:28.300 --> 01:12:31.250
but I was assaulted one
summer when I was here

01:12:31.250 --> 01:12:32.800
for doing theater,

01:12:32.800 --> 01:12:34.390
and it was summer twilight,

01:12:34.390 --> 01:12:37.410
and a bunch of teenage boys came

01:12:37.410 --> 01:12:38.260
and knocked me down

01:12:38.260 --> 01:12:39.730
and started undressing me,

01:12:39.730 --> 01:12:43.720
and it wasn't until I realized
that I needed to scream

01:12:43.720 --> 01:12:46.830
that they left as soon as I screamed,

01:12:46.830 --> 01:12:49.000
and then no one came,

01:12:49.000 --> 01:12:52.860
and so I redressed myself
and went on to my rehearsal.

01:12:52.860 --> 01:12:57.860
Anyway, okay, so but I really
want to talk about feminism,

01:12:59.180 --> 01:13:01.723
because I think like many of you,

01:13:03.390 --> 01:13:06.660
the first year immediately
upon arriving at Yale,

01:13:06.660 --> 01:13:09.090
there was a feeling of being woke,

01:13:09.090 --> 01:13:13.250
especially after the
experiences of the strike.

01:13:13.250 --> 01:13:15.800
I mean, even though that was focused

01:13:15.800 --> 01:13:17.450
on other political issues,

01:13:17.450 --> 01:13:19.960
certainly feminism was there,

01:13:19.960 --> 01:13:22.810
or feminist issues were interwoven

01:13:22.810 --> 01:13:25.770
with all of what was
happening during the strike,

01:13:25.770 --> 01:13:27.720
and I felt it very profoundly.

01:13:27.720 --> 01:13:32.513
I would have to say that
my experiences that year

01:13:35.190 --> 01:13:37.760
have become the foundation for pretty much

01:13:37.760 --> 01:13:39.810
everything I've written since,

01:13:39.810 --> 01:13:42.790
which does not mean to say
that any of what I've written

01:13:42.790 --> 01:13:45.250
since has been autobiographical,

01:13:45.250 --> 01:13:49.337
but I was very interested
at that time in androgyny,

01:13:50.580 --> 01:13:53.240
and my first play was about two women

01:13:53.240 --> 01:13:56.550
who have 10 androgynous
children and send them

01:13:56.550 --> 01:13:57.383
into the world,

01:13:57.383 --> 01:13:58.320
and then they come back

01:13:58.320 --> 01:14:00.120
and report their experiences.

01:14:00.120 --> 01:14:03.890
And I think that that play emerged from,

01:14:03.890 --> 01:14:08.070
now probably those kids would
be gender fluid or whatever,

01:14:08.070 --> 01:14:09.783
but I think at the time,

01:14:11.270 --> 01:14:14.360
that was my personal reaction to being

01:14:14.360 --> 01:14:17.230
in this male environment

01:14:17.230 --> 01:14:20.640
where to be ultra-female,

01:14:20.640 --> 01:14:22.260
I didn't want to be ultra-female,

01:14:22.260 --> 01:14:24.200
but I didn't want to be male either,

01:14:24.200 --> 01:14:27.580
and so exploring the interstices there

01:14:27.580 --> 01:14:29.630
and trying to figure out what that meant.

01:14:30.850 --> 01:14:35.010
I also want to say that I had
a very powerful mentorship

01:14:35.010 --> 01:14:39.050
with the playwright Adrienne Kennedy

01:14:39.050 --> 01:14:44.050
who was having a lot
of success in the '60s.

01:14:44.605 --> 01:14:47.360
"The Funnyhouse of a Negro" was
one of her well known plays,

01:14:47.360 --> 01:14:51.310
and she was really helpful
in helping me get my plays

01:14:51.310 --> 01:14:52.760
produced in New York,

01:14:52.760 --> 01:14:57.760
and there was a kind of underlying
understanding on her part

01:14:58.150 --> 01:15:00.180
that women needed extra help,

01:15:00.180 --> 01:15:04.930
and at any rate, I never
would have hesitated,

01:15:04.930 --> 01:15:09.060
even at that first year, to
say that I was a feminist,

01:15:09.060 --> 01:15:11.540
and I have felt that that never wavered.

01:15:11.540 --> 01:15:13.710
I have been a feminist ever since.

01:15:13.710 --> 01:15:14.680
- Thanks, Cai.

01:15:14.680 --> 01:15:18.070
(audience applauding)

01:15:18.070 --> 01:15:19.460
Kay, you want to speak to that?

01:15:19.460 --> 01:15:20.490
- Sure.

01:15:20.490 --> 01:15:22.550
I have been reflecting,

01:15:22.550 --> 01:15:26.190
including with the other
panels that we've listened to,

01:15:26.190 --> 01:15:28.670
about was I a feminist

01:15:28.670 --> 01:15:30.270
before I ever came to Yale?

01:15:30.270 --> 01:15:33.220
I certainly consider myself one now,

01:15:33.220 --> 01:15:35.350
and I think the answer is yes.

01:15:35.350 --> 01:15:39.330
I think it springs out of
just a belief that we are all

01:15:39.330 --> 01:15:40.580
of equal value,

01:15:40.580 --> 01:15:41.980
and certainly I am,

01:15:41.980 --> 01:15:44.300
and nobody pushes me around,

01:15:44.300 --> 01:15:47.120
and when I was about 12,

01:15:47.120 --> 01:15:49.967
I had a rubber stamp made that said,

01:15:49.967 --> 01:15:52.610
"This ad insults women,"

01:15:52.610 --> 01:15:57.610
and I would stamp these
Ladies Home Journal, McCalls--

01:15:58.440 --> 01:15:59.358
- Go, Kay.

01:15:59.358 --> 01:16:00.191
Go, Kay--

01:16:00.191 --> 01:16:01.024
- And mail it off--

01:16:01.024 --> 01:16:01.857
- You go, Kay--

01:16:01.857 --> 01:16:04.450
- And the amazing thing, I have to find it

01:16:04.450 --> 01:16:05.850
'cause I think I saved it,

01:16:05.850 --> 01:16:07.910
is I actually got a letter

01:16:07.910 --> 01:16:12.910
from John Mack Carter who said,
"Yeah, you're kind of right,

01:16:13.857 --> 01:16:17.267
"but these ads are what
pays for our magazine,"

01:16:18.110 --> 01:16:20.640
and I wasn't gonna take that,

01:16:20.640 --> 01:16:24.030
but I just think it's
interesting he took the time

01:16:24.030 --> 01:16:25.210
to write to a girl,

01:16:25.210 --> 01:16:27.940
and ironically years later,

01:16:27.940 --> 01:16:32.310
there was a feminist sit in in his office,

01:16:32.310 --> 01:16:35.190
so that was quite interesting.

01:16:35.190 --> 01:16:39.720
I was lucky at Yale
though to have a number

01:16:39.720 --> 01:16:43.070
of wonderful women professors,

01:16:43.070 --> 01:16:46.130
and I was sort of surprised to hear

01:16:46.130 --> 01:16:48.810
that some of you had none at all.

01:16:48.810 --> 01:16:53.810
I just wanted to recognize
Ann Hanson in History of Art.

01:16:54.125 --> 01:16:56.210
(audience applauding)

01:16:56.210 --> 01:17:00.173
Ruth Day, she was wonderful in psychology.

01:17:01.860 --> 01:17:05.370
Kerala Snyder, I had for music theory,

01:17:05.370 --> 01:17:08.350
and she was a Wellesley woman.

01:17:08.350 --> 01:17:10.710
As soon as I left
Wellesley, I was convinced

01:17:10.710 --> 01:17:13.680
I was meeting a remarkable Wellesley woman

01:17:13.680 --> 01:17:15.163
everywhere I turned,

01:17:16.000 --> 01:17:17.690
which would say, well, why did you leave?

01:17:17.690 --> 01:17:19.970
Including Hilary Clinton

01:17:19.970 --> 01:17:22.420
I had known at Wellesley
because she was a senior.

01:17:22.420 --> 01:17:25.230
I was a freshman, and she
was always in our dorm,

01:17:25.230 --> 01:17:27.133
an amazing woman I looked up to,

01:17:28.540 --> 01:17:33.350
and then Ellen Keniston,
also here in the Law School.

01:17:33.350 --> 01:17:37.920
I took a great course about women and law.

01:17:37.920 --> 01:17:40.060
I know one of teachers was Ann Hill.

01:17:40.060 --> 01:17:41.550
I don't remember the name of the other,

01:17:41.550 --> 01:17:43.320
but it was a terrific seminar,

01:17:43.320 --> 01:17:45.073
so I felt very nurtured,

01:17:46.230 --> 01:17:51.230
and I think the literature
that we had already read,

01:17:52.260 --> 01:17:54.300
but also were exposed to,

01:17:54.300 --> 01:17:57.690
before I came, I know I
had read Betty Freidan.

01:17:57.690 --> 01:18:00.290
I think here, Simone deBeauvoir.

01:18:00.290 --> 01:18:02.520
Kate Millett actually worked out

01:18:02.520 --> 01:18:06.400
of my father's radical
church in New York City

01:18:06.400 --> 01:18:10.070
on Washington Square and
then Susan Brown Miller.

01:18:10.070 --> 01:18:12.980
Now I still like feminist reading,

01:18:12.980 --> 01:18:15.550
but I'm going more towards humor

01:18:15.550 --> 01:18:18.680
'cause I think there is humor in feminism.

01:18:18.680 --> 01:18:22.520
A book that came out about
two years ago I loved

01:18:22.520 --> 01:18:24.350
was "How to be a Woman"

01:18:24.350 --> 01:18:27.470
by a British writer, Caitlin Moran.

01:18:27.470 --> 01:18:30.260
If you have a chance, it's very funny

01:18:30.260 --> 01:18:31.950
and will teach you how to be a woman.

01:18:31.950 --> 01:18:33.778
It's never too late,

01:18:33.778 --> 01:18:37.010
(audience laughing)

01:18:37.010 --> 01:18:38.915
and then another thought I had,

01:18:38.915 --> 01:18:42.410
I have always been involved
in the women's movement,

01:18:42.410 --> 01:18:45.950
but I was envious of those among us

01:18:45.950 --> 01:18:50.160
who were more vocal, louder.

01:18:50.160 --> 01:18:52.340
I really looked up to them.

01:18:52.340 --> 01:18:57.240
I also one time edging
into the sexual harassment,

01:18:57.240 --> 01:19:01.320
a TA who loved my writing
and would embarrass me

01:19:01.320 --> 01:19:03.010
having me read it out loud,

01:19:03.010 --> 01:19:05.023
my papers to the class,

01:19:06.090 --> 01:19:09.120
invited me to a party at his house,

01:19:09.120 --> 01:19:12.900
and I immediately thought,
he grades my papers.

01:19:12.900 --> 01:19:14.800
I can't go to his party,

01:19:14.800 --> 01:19:18.643
and I told him, and I'm just amazed now.

01:19:20.750 --> 01:19:22.793
I don't know where that sense came from,

01:19:25.410 --> 01:19:29.121
and so still a feminist.

01:19:29.121 --> 01:19:29.954
- Thank you.

01:19:29.954 --> 01:19:33.121
(audience applauding)

01:19:34.050 --> 01:19:34.883
Forgive me.

01:19:34.883 --> 01:19:39.100
We have just 10 minutes
left and three questions,

01:19:39.100 --> 01:19:40.690
and so I'm just gonna,

01:19:40.690 --> 01:19:43.380
to kind of give you a breadth here,

01:19:43.380 --> 01:19:47.340
I am gonna just move on
to the next question,

01:19:47.340 --> 01:19:49.330
and hopefully we'll have
time to pull the audience in,

01:19:49.330 --> 01:19:50.970
but this is one that has come up,

01:19:50.970 --> 01:19:55.210
which is the question
of sexual harassment.

01:19:55.210 --> 01:19:58.060
That term sexual harassment
was not even coined yet.

01:19:58.060 --> 01:20:02.690
No one even knew that phrase
when you all arrived here,

01:20:02.690 --> 01:20:07.330
but that doesn't mean
that students were immune

01:20:07.330 --> 01:20:09.780
to sexual harassment,
whether by professors

01:20:09.780 --> 01:20:13.283
or graduate students or other students,

01:20:14.250 --> 01:20:15.790
although when it happened,

01:20:15.790 --> 01:20:18.260
typically it was kept very quiet,

01:20:18.260 --> 01:20:20.880
so you may never have even
known it was going on,

01:20:20.880 --> 01:20:23.300
even if it was with the person,

01:20:23.300 --> 01:20:24.400
someone you knew.

01:20:24.400 --> 01:20:26.910
Joan, you had said you'd
speak to this question.

01:20:26.910 --> 01:20:28.498
Do you want to talk to us about it?

01:20:28.498 --> 01:20:31.430
- I'd like to speak to this
question because all my life,

01:20:31.430 --> 01:20:36.393
since Yale I have regretted
not speaking about it.

01:20:37.860 --> 01:20:38.693
Something happened.

01:20:38.693 --> 01:20:40.410
I kept it to myself,

01:20:40.410 --> 01:20:42.460
so I was a freshman.

01:20:42.460 --> 01:20:44.370
I'm bright eyed.

01:20:44.370 --> 01:20:49.140
I'm having this thrilling
intellectual experience at Yale,

01:20:49.140 --> 01:20:51.510
and one of my professors,

01:20:51.510 --> 01:20:55.040
who I admired and has since gone on

01:20:55.040 --> 01:20:57.913
to be a great, world,

01:20:59.160 --> 01:21:02.190
he had assigned weekly papers,

01:21:02.190 --> 01:21:03.920
and I was getting As on my papers,

01:21:03.920 --> 01:21:06.130
which was thrilling because I was worried,

01:21:06.130 --> 01:21:08.700
like everyone else, that I
was gonna fail out of Yale,

01:21:08.700 --> 01:21:12.540
or I didn't know if I was
qualified or whatever.

01:21:12.540 --> 01:21:17.540
So about the third, fourth week,

01:21:18.140 --> 01:21:19.310
I get a paper back,

01:21:19.310 --> 01:21:22.380
and after getting As, this one had a C,

01:21:22.380 --> 01:21:27.380
and it had a note on it saying, "See me,"

01:21:27.883 --> 01:21:28.850
(laughing)

01:21:28.850 --> 01:21:31.890
so I made an appointment,

01:21:31.890 --> 01:21:33.690
and I went to his office.

01:21:33.690 --> 01:21:34.810
I had to figure out where it was.

01:21:34.810 --> 01:21:37.760
I had never gone to a professor's office,

01:21:37.760 --> 01:21:39.327
and he started talking to me,

01:21:39.327 --> 01:21:40.360
and he got up,

01:21:40.360 --> 01:21:43.180
and he locked the door to his office,

01:21:43.180 --> 01:21:47.510
and then he chased me around the room,

01:21:47.510 --> 01:21:49.530
and I was floored.

01:21:49.530 --> 01:21:51.438
I was like, what?

01:21:51.438 --> 01:21:53.120
I wasn't scared.

01:21:53.120 --> 01:21:55.770
I was just nonplussed.

01:21:55.770 --> 01:21:56.620
I was like, what?

01:21:56.620 --> 01:21:57.590
You're my professor.

01:21:57.590 --> 01:21:59.150
What the hell are you doing?

01:21:59.150 --> 01:22:01.370
I was like, I didn't get it,

01:22:01.370 --> 01:22:05.780
and I was like, just couldn't believe it.

01:22:05.780 --> 01:22:07.220
Anyway, this went on,

01:22:07.220 --> 01:22:09.160
and finally I just yelled at him,

01:22:09.160 --> 01:22:10.610
and I said, let me out of here.

01:22:10.610 --> 01:22:11.730
I'm just gonna scream.

01:22:11.730 --> 01:22:13.600
Just let me out of here now.

01:22:13.600 --> 01:22:14.600
I left.

01:22:14.600 --> 01:22:18.010
Okay, so then I told a couple of people

01:22:18.010 --> 01:22:20.780
just because I was kind of traumatized,

01:22:20.780 --> 01:22:24.320
so I go back to class,
write my next weekly paper,

01:22:24.320 --> 01:22:26.650
and I came back with an F on it.

01:22:26.650 --> 01:22:27.750
- Oh, no.

01:22:27.750 --> 01:22:30.307
- And not only that, it
had a comment saying,

01:22:30.307 --> 01:22:32.837
"This is the best paper
you've written all year,"

01:22:33.970 --> 01:22:37.860
so when that happened,
I wasn't really worried

01:22:37.860 --> 01:22:40.060
because I had all this evidence.

01:22:40.060 --> 01:22:41.470
I mean, what an idiot,

01:22:41.470 --> 01:22:45.250
so I just kept all the
papers in a little file.

01:22:45.250 --> 01:22:46.760
I wrote my next paper.

01:22:46.760 --> 01:22:48.370
I think I got a D or an F

01:22:48.370 --> 01:22:49.940
on every other paper.

01:22:49.940 --> 01:22:52.596
Finally, it comes to blue
book day where you're sitting

01:22:52.596 --> 01:22:55.710
to take your final for this course,

01:22:55.710 --> 01:22:57.790
and he's at the podium

01:22:57.790 --> 01:23:00.450
addressing everyone about the test,

01:23:00.450 --> 01:23:03.730
and before he left, I
got up out of my seat,

01:23:03.730 --> 01:23:05.550
and I went up to the podium with my back

01:23:05.550 --> 01:23:06.617
to the audience here,

01:23:06.617 --> 01:23:08.810
to the other students, and I said,

01:23:08.810 --> 01:23:10.600
excuse me, sir.

01:23:10.600 --> 01:23:11.680
Shall I take the test,

01:23:11.680 --> 01:23:13.180
or are you just gonna fail me?

01:23:14.430 --> 01:23:16.090
And he looked at me,

01:23:16.090 --> 01:23:18.460
and he said, "Take the test,"

01:23:18.460 --> 01:23:21.680
so I took the test, and that was it,

01:23:21.680 --> 01:23:24.130
and I think I got an A in
the course or something,

01:23:24.130 --> 01:23:25.570
an A minus or something,

01:23:25.570 --> 01:23:28.540
but it was like so ridiculous,

01:23:28.540 --> 01:23:31.620
and I thought I should have done something

01:23:31.620 --> 01:23:34.330
because I don't know if other
people were going through

01:23:34.330 --> 01:23:35.630
things like that,

01:23:35.630 --> 01:23:37.910
but it was like this kind of weird thing,

01:23:37.910 --> 01:23:42.270
and the professor had never
had a student who was a woman,

01:23:42.270 --> 01:23:44.423
had never been in class with a woman.

01:23:45.610 --> 01:23:48.670
I think I felt, should I wreck his career?

01:23:48.670 --> 01:23:50.960
Should I make this into a big deal?

01:23:50.960 --> 01:23:54.250
Or is it he's just as
clueless as I was, you know?

01:23:54.250 --> 01:23:57.000
I felt like we were
both kind of mirroring,

01:23:57.000 --> 01:24:01.400
like I was coming into a
situation I didn't understand,

01:24:01.400 --> 01:24:03.270
and I think he was as well,

01:24:03.270 --> 01:24:05.300
but I've been conflicted really.

01:24:05.300 --> 01:24:06.730
I think about it all the time,

01:24:06.730 --> 01:24:09.910
and with this Me Too movement even more,

01:24:09.910 --> 01:24:13.223
but I wonder if other people
had experiences like that.

01:24:14.772 --> 01:24:15.980
- Thank you for sharing that.

01:24:15.980 --> 01:24:17.953
It's not always easy to talk about.

01:24:19.810 --> 01:24:21.550
We only have five minutes left.

01:24:21.550 --> 01:24:22.440
I'm so sorry.

01:24:22.440 --> 01:24:24.133
This time has just flown.

01:24:25.100 --> 01:24:27.377
Let me tell you,

01:24:27.377 --> 01:24:29.070
and I apologize to the panelists

01:24:29.070 --> 01:24:32.910
'cause they've prepared
for some other questions

01:24:32.910 --> 01:24:34.713
that we simply don't have time for,

01:24:35.753 --> 01:24:39.130
but I want to share those
with you because I do hope

01:24:39.130 --> 01:24:41.513
you continue this conversation.

01:24:42.530 --> 01:24:44.710
One is on women who left,

01:24:44.710 --> 01:24:48.310
so my stats are just on the freshman class

01:24:48.310 --> 01:24:51.640
'cause those were, from a
researcher's point of view,

01:24:51.640 --> 01:24:53.360
the cleanest stats to look at,

01:24:53.360 --> 01:24:56.300
but roughly 10% of the women who started

01:24:56.300 --> 01:24:57.737
as freshman here in 1969

01:24:59.760 --> 01:25:01.870
chose to leave before
graduating from Yale,

01:25:01.870 --> 01:25:05.180
which is the exact same
percentage as the men,

01:25:05.180 --> 01:25:06.680
so given all that you went through,

01:25:06.680 --> 01:25:08.513
that's actually pretty remarkable.

01:25:09.929 --> 01:25:12.470
And then number of women,

01:25:12.470 --> 01:25:14.180
you may have heard that today,

01:25:14.180 --> 01:25:16.410
figured out how to create
some room from themselves,

01:25:16.410 --> 01:25:18.640
either by going on the five year BA

01:25:18.640 --> 01:25:23.300
or taking a term abroad or
dropping out for a semester

01:25:23.300 --> 01:25:26.666
and then coming back, and
so that was one question.

01:25:26.666 --> 01:25:29.200
Did you know anyone who left Yale,

01:25:29.200 --> 01:25:31.350
and if so how did that affect you,

01:25:31.350 --> 01:25:33.110
or did you ever consider

01:25:33.110 --> 01:25:35.370
leaving Yale yourself?

01:25:35.370 --> 01:25:39.870
And then the ninth question,
the closing question,

01:25:39.870 --> 01:25:44.580
what was the best or funniest
or most unexpected part

01:25:44.580 --> 01:25:45.860
about being at Yale,

01:25:45.860 --> 01:25:49.270
so I encourage you to ask one
another that this evening,

01:25:49.270 --> 01:25:52.280
and what was the hardest or the worst?

01:25:52.280 --> 01:25:56.130
So anyway, as I said, this
was mission impossible.

01:25:56.130 --> 01:25:59.080
So many important stories
out there to hear,

01:25:59.080 --> 01:26:02.100
and we have just skimmed the surface,

01:26:02.100 --> 01:26:05.640
but I do want to close not
just by thanking this panel,

01:26:05.640 --> 01:26:08.920
but by thanking all of
you and thanking you,

01:26:08.920 --> 01:26:11.550
as a woman who came after you.

01:26:11.550 --> 01:26:15.530
Thank you for so much for
what you did for all of us,

01:26:15.530 --> 01:26:18.130
making it easier to be a woman at Yale,

01:26:18.130 --> 01:26:20.020
so why don't we close
with a round of applause

01:26:20.020 --> 01:26:21.518
for you and for our panel?

01:26:21.518 --> 01:26:24.685
(audience applauding)

01:26:27.242 --> 01:26:28.913
(mumbling)

